ency.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r5s0 (improvement, +13.88%): Registering the full forwarded rs1 + immediate sum in EX/MEM.effective_addr isolates RAM addressing and byte-lane logic from the multiplier/general ALU result mux, raising median Fmax to 54.331 MHz without changing CoreMark's 4,201,709 cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r5s1 (regression, +13.41%): Sharing MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU behind operand/result registers raises Fmax, but two extra EX wait cycles per multiply add 4.46% CoreMark cycles, leaving it slower than isolating EX/MEM.effective_addr without added stalls (128.78 versus 129.31 iterations/s).\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r5s2 (regression, +5.78%): Initializing bimodal counters in an initial loop and retaining them across runtime reset removes reset logic and improves Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles, but yields a smaller clock gain than isolating EX/MEM.effective_addr (120.11 versus 129.31 iterations/s).\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261001-001-r6s0 (improvement, +5.50%): Replacing bulk register-file data reset with a written bitmap, distributed RAM attribution and mutually exclusive masked bypass/stored reads restores explicit ID/EX operand registers, removing the BSRAM-to-EX launch path and improving Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
