{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261002-002-r13s1.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r13s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "Wrote and schema-validated [hyp-20261002-002-r13s1.yaml](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-astra_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261002-002-r13s1-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r13s1.yaml).\n\nProposes narrow operand-candidate arithmetic slices with a shared carry tree. Estimated gain: **8%, low confidence**; final design unmeasured. Experimental RTL edits were restored.id: hyp-20261002-002-r13s1\ntitle: Partition EX into operand-candidate arithmetic slices\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |-\n  The champion scores 199.08 iterations/s at 79.422 MHz and 3,989,539 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior r8s1 registered address matches but retained live qualification and priority before arithmetic. The r12 balanced comparator gained only 3.57 percent because arithmetic and recovery paths competed. Local probes confirm that retiming routing alone leaves these paths dominant, while replicating whole ALUs creates excessive area and routing. A structural execution fabric that selects narrow arithmetic summaries before one shared carry tree targets both limitations without extra instruction latency.\nhypothesis: |-\n  Replace the shared mux-then-wide-arithmetic EX topology with a bank of narrow operand-candidate arithmetic slices and one shared carry-prefix network. Register complete operand routes before EX so their matching and priority work is not chained through arithmetic. This is one structural redesign of operand delivery and base execution; preserve the five-stage schedule, one-retire interface, predictor, fetch queue, load-use bubble, memory protocol and blocking M-extension schedules.\n\n  First compute exact next-cycle producer identities and write eligibility at the current ID/EX boundary. Retain the existing next_rs1/next_rs2, next_ex_rd and next_wb_rd rules. Next EX/MEM reg_write is its held value on dmem stall, zero on an EX bubble or execute_wait, otherwise the actual post-trap control that EX will capture. Next MEM/WB reg_write is its held value on hold_mem_wb, otherwise MEM's post-alignm\n…[8853 of 11925 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:51a6dc13993cbd77]…\ne forwarding qualification ports. Preserve hazard\n    timing and external memory/RVFI interfaces with nret=1.\n- file: test/test_pipeline.py\n  description: |-\n    Retain architectural and timing checks while adapting internal\n    forwarding-encoding assertions to one-hot routes and exact values.\n    Add route-transition tests covering competing/faulting writers,\n    every hold/flush/reset combination, load-use and M waits, x0,\n    PC/immediate overrides and original raw RVFI/store operands.\n     Include any new RTL module in this allowed test runner source list. Exercise slice carry/compare results through forwarding, branches and stalls without weakening architectural assertions.\n- file: test/test_execute_slice.py\n  description: |-\n    Add independent reference checks for candidate selection and ADD/SUB/SLT/SLTU plus all branch predicates, nibble carry/borrow boundaries, signed extremes, original-operand equality and randomized values. Exercise raw and PC/immediate routes and exact one-hot route assumptions.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
