{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r6s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Localize bimodal counter updates without changing prediction policy'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 129.31 iterations/s at 54.331 MHz with 4,201,709\nbracketed CoreMark cycles. Its measured worst path runs through EX\nbranch recovery and fetch logic. The 64-entry predictor currently\nreads counters[train_index] through an indexed mux, computes a shared\nsaturating increment/decrement, and distributes the result back to\nan indexed write. Prior work found useful timing gains from reducing\ncounter reset logic, but altered counter lifetime. This micro_opt\ninstead removes the shared update-data mux while preserving reset,\nstate transitions, prediction policy, and every accepted instruction.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Implement each of the existing 64 two-bit counters as a locally\nupdated register. Use a fixed generate index i; reset counters[i] to\n2'b10 and otherwise enable it only when train_valid && train_index==i.\nCompute its next state directly from its own pre-edge bits and the\nshared train_taken bit. For old state {h,l}, taken gives\n{h|l, h|\"'!l}; not-taken gives {h&l, h&!l}. These are the exact two-bit\nsaturating increment/decrement truth tables, including held 00/11\nat saturation. Each counter has its own small update cone; there\nis no indexed counter read for computing the update data.\n\nKeep counters as the same 64-entry array for the existing asynchronous\nfetch lookup. Preserve synchronous reset to weakly taken, PC[7:2]\nindexing, pre-edge lookup when training aliases the fetch index,\naccepted legal conditional-branch-only training, and training hold\nunder reset or pipeline stalls. Do not change prediction accuracy,\ncounter state lifetime, target calculation, recovery, or the fetch\ninterface. This is an implementation optimization of an existing\nstate machine, not a new predictor or a larger history table.\n\nAll data/control pipeline registers, divider/multiplier timing,\nforwarding, dedicated effective addresses, memory handshakes, decoder\nlegality, nret=1 ports, and RVFI behavior stay as before. Keep the\noriginal sat-counter array observable in unit tests and retain exact\nsaturation, once-only training, and pre-edge alias checks.\n\nValidate all eight state/direction transitions, unselected-entry\nholds, reset priority, and same-index simultaneous lookup/training.\nRun the existing predictor and pipeline tests, unmodified formal and\nstalled cosimulation, and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks.\nExpect 4,201,709 bracketed cycles; acceptance requires more than\n135.25826 iterations/s, equivalent to 56.8316 MHz at that count.\nThe predicted clock gain is not yet measured. Distributed update\nlogic can increase train-signal fanout, and other arithmetic paths\nmay limit the final median Gowin Fmax.\n'\"'''\nd['changes']=[{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Replace the shared dynamically indexed counter update with 64\nstatically indexed clocked update processes, each using local\nsaturating next-state boolean equations and a decoded train enable.\nPreserve the array's fetch lookup, reset values and priority, exact\ncounter transitions, nonblocking pre-edge semantics, and every\nother prediction/PC-control behavior.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve all existing tests and exercise every counter state in\nboth training directions, all index selections, saturation holds,\nreset priority, simultaneous aliasing fetch/training, and periods\nwith no accepted branch. Keep exact prediction/training and RVFI\nassertions under independent instruction/data bus stalls.\n'''}]\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\nDumper.add_representer(str,lambda du,v: du.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',v,style='|' if '\\\\n' in v else None))\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and validated hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r6s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Localize bimodal counter updates without changing prediction policy'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 129.31 iterations/s at 54.331 MHz with 4,201,709\nbracketed CoreMark cycles. Its measured worst path runs through EX\nbranch recovery and fetch logic. The 64-entry predictor currently\nreads counters[train_index] through an indexed mux, computes a shared\nsaturating increment/decrement, and distributes the result back to\nan indexed write. Prior work found useful timing gains from reducing\ncounter reset logic, but altered counter lifetime. This micro_opt\ninstead removes the shared update-data mux while preserving reset,\nstate transitions, prediction policy, and every accepted instruction.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Implement each of the existing 64 two-bit counters as a locally\nupdated register. Use a fixed generate index i; reset counters[i] to\n2'b10 and otherwise enable it only when train_valid && train_index==i.\nCompute its next state directly from its own pre-edge bits and the\nshared train_taken bit. For old state {h,l}, taken gives\n{h|l, h|\"'!l}; not-taken gives {h&l, h&!l}. These are the exact two-bit\nsaturating increment/decrement truth tables, including held 00/11\nat saturation. Each counter has its own small update cone; there\nis no indexed counter read for computing the update data.\n\nKeep counters as the same 64-entry array for the existing asynchronous\nfetch lookup. Preserve synchronous reset to weakly taken, PC[7:2]\nindexing, pre-edge lookup when training aliases the fetch index,\naccepted legal conditional-branch-only training, and training hold\nunder reset or pipeline stalls. Do not change prediction accuracy,\ncounter state lifetime, target calculation, recovery, or the fetch\ninterface. This is an implementation optimization of an existing\nstate machine, not a new predictor or a larger history table.\n\nAll data/control pipeline registers, divider/multiplier timing,\nforwarding, dedicated effective addresses, memory handshakes, decoder\nlegality, nret=1 ports, and RVFI behavior stay as before. Keep the\noriginal sat-counter array observable in unit tests and retain exact\nsaturation, once-only training, and pre-edge alias checks.\n\nValidate all eight state/direction transitions, unselected-entry\nholds, reset priority, and same-index simultaneous lookup/training.\nRun the existing predictor and pipeline tests, unmodified formal and\nstalled cosimulation, and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks.\nExpect 4,201,709 bracketed cycles; acceptance requires more than\n135.25826 iterations/s, equivalent to 56.8316 MHz at that count.\nThe predicted clock gain is not yet measured. Distributed update\nlogic can increase train-signal fanout, and other arithmetic paths\nmay limit the final median Gowin Fmax.\n'\"'''\nd['changes']=[{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Replace the shared dynamically indexed counter update with 64\nstatically indexed clocked update processes, each using local\nsaturating next-state boolean equations and a decoded train enable.\nPreserve the array's fetch lookup, reset values and priority, exact\ncounter transitions, nonblocking pre-edge semantics, and every\nother prediction/PC-control behavior.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve all existing tests and exercise every counter state in\nboth training directions, all index selections, saturation holds,\nreset priority, simultaneous aliasing fetch/training, and periods\nwith no accepted branch. Keep exact prediction/training and RVFI\nassertions under independent instruction/data bus stalls.\n'''}]\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\nDumper.add_representer(str,lambda du,v: du.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',v,style='|' if '\\\\n' in v else None))\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and validated hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Updated and validated hypothesis\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
