{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_66", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r6s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Shorten radix-4 divider feedback to two digits per clock'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 129.31 iterations/s at 54.331 MHz with 4,201,709\nbracketed CoreMark cycles. Fresh timing reproduces divider feedback\nwithin 0.034 ns of the worst placement-0 path: 25.480 ns through\nthree dependent radix-4 subtract/select stages. Prior micro_opt r2s0\nsuccessfully traded a lower divider unroll factor for a faster global\nclock; r1s1 and r2s0 both reported 4,491,485 bracketed cycles despite\ntheir different divide latencies. The present three-digit radix-4\nfeedback can be shortened without changing other execution paths.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Reduce the existing radix-4 divider's combinational recurrence from\nthree digits per clock to two. Preserve operand normalization,\nregistered 3D, parallel D/2D/3D trial subtractions, one-hot remainder\nselection, and registered sign/exception correction. Keep the first\npeeled radix-4 digit on the same edge that registers 3D. Then perform\nseven iterations of two digits and a final iteration of one digit:\n1 + 7*2 + 1 = 16 digits, exactly 32 dividend bits. The final cycle\nmust select quotient_step[1]/remainder_step[1], never a second digit.\n\nUse a four-bit iteration counter: index 0 peels the first digit and\nregisters 3D; indexes 1 through 7 use the second recurrence output;\nindex 8 uses the first recurrence output and enters CORRECT. Capture\noperands on edge 1, execute these nine iteration clocks on edges\n2 through 10, and register correction/result_valid on edge 11. The\nearliest EX/MEM transfer is edge 12. Preserve held completion and\nthe once-only request/transfer protocol. The shorter recurrence has\nsix parallel trial subtractors instead of nine, reducing arithmetic\narea and serial remainder dependence at the cost of three additional\nblocking clocks per divide. Do not change radix, normalize differently,\nor add variable-latency special cases.\n\nUse exactly the same schedule and handshake in RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS;\nretain all four exact XOR formulas. Preserve real RV32M division by\nzero, signed INT_MIN/-1, truncation toward zero and remainder sign.\nNo other instruction latency, branch/predictor policy, memory address\npath, forwarding, pipeline interface, retirement width, or invariant\nchanges. Existing EX logic must continue holding the divide while\nolder instructions drain, with dmem hold taking priority.\n\nUpdate exact-latency assertions to eleven internal clocks and earliest\nedge-twelve transfer in both arithmetic builds, rather than removing\nlatency checks. Test every radix-4 digit position, the last partial\niteration, signs, exceptional values, held completion and reset during\neach phase. Retain pipeline tests for saved forwarded operands,\nback-to-back divides, dependencies, bus stalls and once-only retirement.\nRun unmodified formal, stalled reference cosimulation and canonical\nCoreMark CRC/UART/marker validation. Confirm whether bracketed cycles\nremain 4,201,709 rather than assuming divisions are outside the bracket.\nAcceptance requires fitness above 135.25826 iterations/s, or more than\n56.8316 MHz if cycles are unchanged. The expected gain remains\nunmeasured; branch recovery/multiply timing and the longer formal\nlatency are the main risks. Never relax formal depths or liveness.\n'''\nd['changes']=[{'file':'rtl/divider.sv','description':'''Instantiate two radix-4 recurrence stages, widen iteration_q to\nfour bits, and implement the 1+7*2+1 digit schedule with the last\niteration selecting step[1]. Preserve registered 3D, signed handling,\nexception correction, all handshake/reset behavior, and exact\nALTOPS arithmetic with the same eleven-clock internal latency.\n'''},{'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'''Update divider-latency documentation to eleven internal clocks and\nnext-available-edge transfer. Keep the ALU datapath, multiplier,\nbase operations, and divider interface unchanged.\n'''},{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Update divide-latency documentation to eleven internal clocks and\nearliest edge-twelve transfer; retain the existing blocking request,\ncompletion, saved-operand and forwarding logic without new states.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'''Retain all real-arithmetic and ALTOPS vectors. Update exact latency\nchecks to eleven internal clocks, extend reset-phase coverage, and\ncheck the final single-digit iteration with values differing only\nin the low two dividend bits and all possible final quotient digits.\nPreserve held-result and no-relaunch assertions.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all existing architectural, predictor, and memory checks.\nUpdate only exact divide-latency expectations to the longer defined\nschedule and extend bounded test cycle budgets if needed. Exercise\nconsecutive/dependent divides, forwarded launch operands, mixed\noperations, stalled completion, reset cancellation, wrong-path\nsuppression, and strict once-only retirement and side effects.\n'''}]\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\nDumper.add_representer(str,lambda du,v: du.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',v,style='|' if '\\\\n' in v else None))\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and validated hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
