{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_86", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r6s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Map the register file to distributed RAM with reset validity bits'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 129.31 iterations/s at 54.331 MHz and 4,201,709\nbracketed CoreMark cycles. A fresh Gowin run reproduced its 25.514 ns\nworst path starting at the register-file BSRAM output and traversing\noperand selection and branch recovery. Other local arithmetic/control\nprototypes likewise expose register-file-to-EX paths. The present\n32-by-32 array has a bulk synchronous reset and is mapped into block\nRAM with pipeline register absorption. Changing its storage mapping\nwhile preserving its exact logical interface is a micro_opt opportunity.\n'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Implement the same 32-register, two-asynchronous-read, single-write\nregister file with distributed RAM storage and a small resettable\nwritten bitmap. Remove only the bulk reset assignment to the 32-bit\ndata words. Apply the Gowin synthesis attribute\n(* syn_ramstyle = \\\"distributed_ram\\\" *) to the regs array, retaining\nportable behavioral RTL for simulation and formal verification.\nKeep one synchronous data write when \"'!reset && w_en && w_addr!=0.\n\nReset written_q to zero; every accepted nonzero-register write sets\nwritten_q[w_addr]. Each read retains the original priority: x0 reads\nzero; a matching enabled write bypasses w_data; otherwise return the\nstored word only when that register'\"'s written bit is set, and zero\nwhen it is clear. Preserve the original bypass behavior even during\nreset rather than silently changing the combinational interface.\nReset suppresses data writes and clears validity, so stale RAM words\ncannot leak after reset. Every valid word must have been written\nsince the most recent reset. Never assume uninitialized RAM is zero.\n\nThe desired physical result is distributed LUT RAM plus explicit ID/EX\noperand registers, eliminating the block-RAM output as the launch\npoint of the long EX arithmetic/recovery paths. Verify this mapping\nin the vendor report; the attribute alone is not evidence of success.\nThis changes storage/reset implementation only: preserve register\naddresses, two read ports, one write port, x0, write-first bypass,\nzero-after-reset semantics, ID/EX stall capture, all forwarding,\noperation latencies, divider and multiplier logic, prediction policy,\neffective addresses, memory/RVFI ports, and nret=1. No initial block,\nstartup sweep, pipeline stage, or program-specific specialization.\n\nRetain all existing register-file and pipeline tests. Exercise writes\nto every register, both simultaneous reads, x0 writes, both write-first\nbypass paths, reset after nonzero data, reads before first post-reset\nwrite, reset/write coincidence, and different read/write addresses.\nVerify dependent arithmetic, branches, stores and divider launches\nunder independent bus stalls, including retained WB forwarding.\nRun unchanged formal and stalled cosimulation and the canonical\nCoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks. Expect the same 4,201,709 bracketed\ncycles. Acceptance requires fitness above 135.25826 iterations/s,\nor more than 56.8316 MHz at that cycle count. This is an unmeasured\nprediction; RAM mapping, added read-valid muxes, fetch/decode timing,\nand remaining divider feedback can limit the gain.\n'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':10,'confidence':'medium'}\nd['changes']=[{'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Use a distributed-RAM inference attribute on the unchanged 32x32\nstorage array, remove its bulk reset, and add per-register written\nbits with synchronous reset and write tracking. Mask unwritten\nreads to zero after applying the original x0 and same-cycle bypass\npriorities. Preserve ports and all write/reset timing semantics.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Keep all existing tests and add repeated reset after filling every\nregister, unwritten-register reads, reset/write coincidence, x0\nsuppression, and concurrent two-port/bypass checks. Use an independent\narchitectural zero-reset model; never rely on raw RAM initialization.\n'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all current assertions and add repeated-reset execution and\nregister dependencies using ALU/load/WB forwarding, branch/JALR,\nstores and blocking divide operands under independent bus stalls.\nCheck exact RVFI source values/results, PC sequence, retirement\norder, and no stale pre-reset data or side effects.\n'''}]\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\nDumper.add_representer(str,lambda du,v: du.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',v,style='|' if '\\\\n' in v else None))\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and validated hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
