{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_74", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-002-r6s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-002-r6s1\ntitle: \\\"Peel four dividend bits at launch and shorten the divider recurrence\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 129.31 iterations/s at median 54.331 MHz and\n  4,201,709 bracketed CoreMark cycles. A fresh unchanged-core Gowin run\n  reproduces its 25.514 ns branch-recovery path and a nearly tied\n  25.480 ns divider feedback path. Earlier structural divider staging\n  produced large gains without changing measured CoreMark cycles.\n  Three serial radix-4 digit selections still occupy each feedback\n  cycle. Shortening this recurrence must preserve the original latency:\n  an extra divide cycle fails the unchanged load-dependent liveness bound.\nhypothesis: |\n  Repartition the shared divider without changing its eight internal\n  clocks or earliest edge-9 result transfer. On request edge 1 normalize\n  signed operands, process the first FOUR dividend bits with a small\n  leading-digit network, and register the full 34-bit 3D multiple.\n  On edges 2 through 8 reuse TWO full-width radix-4 restoring stages per\n  clock. This consumes exactly 4 + 7*4 = 32 dividend bits. In DONE expose\n  sign correction from stable final quotient/remainder registers to the\n  existing EX/MEM acceptance register; remove the separate CORRECT state\n  and corrected-result register. This is one divider-boundary change.\n\n  Let A and D denote normalized 32-bit magnitudes and h=A[31:28]. The\n  launch network computes q_head=floor(h/D) and r_head=h-q_head*D.\n  For D>=16 the head quotient is zero and remainder is h. For D<16,\n  implement two tiny radix-4 digit steps with four-bit remainder/quotient\n  state, six-bit shifted values and D/2D/3D, and seven-bit unsigned trial\n  differences. The low four divisor bits suffice only in this arm.\n  Do not truncate the full divisor used by subsequent iterations. D=0\n  may use deterministic head values because the captured zero flag\n  overrides the final architectural result. Avoid full-width / or %.\n  Register quotient_q={A[27:0],q_head} and remainder_q={28'b0,r_head}\n  at launch, alongside normalized D, 34-bit 3D, original dividend,\n  quotient/remainder sign flags, operation selection, zero and overflow.\n  Compute 3D from D with explicitly widened D + (D<<1), not a 32-bit sum.\n\n  Keep only two dependent full-width radix-4 stages. Each computes\n  parallel unsigned trial differences against registered D, 2D, and 3D,\n  then selects the largest fitting multiple with one-hot masks. Preserve\n  34-bit shifted dividends and 35-bit trial differences with borrow bit\n  34. For iteration indices 0..6, register quotient_step[2] and\n  remainder_step[2]; enter DONE after index 6. Processing fourteen more\n  radix-4 digits after the two at launch consumes exactly sixteen digits.\n\n  In DONE, result_valid is high and result is combinational sign\n  correction/selection of quotient_q or remainder_q, with the captured\n  divide-by-zero and signed-overflow overrides. Do not correct the live\n  iteration outputs: correction starts at the stable state registers.\n  Keep all these registers and flags unchanged while DONE is blocked,\n  so result stays stable until result_ready. EX/MEM captures it on edge\n  9 at the earliest. Reset cancels pending work; consume once and return\n  to IDLE only on acceptance. Preserve signed truncation toward zero,\n  remainder sign, INT_MIN/-1, and all four division operations exactly.\n\n  Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS capture the original operands/op at request,\n  run the same seven iteration clocks, and expose the original four\n  XOR formulas in DONE with the identical edge-9 transfer schedule.\n  Preserve EX's once-only request/completion protocol, saved RVFI source\n  values, downstream draining, memory-hold priority, and full bubbles.\n  Keep multiplication, ordinary ALU operations, prediction, effective\n  memory addresses, nret=1 IO, legality, traps and retirement order intact.\n  Do not alter any formal depth, liveness bound, or existing exact-latency\n  assertion. Keep the current two-bit state encoding with IDLE=0,\n  ITERATE=1 and DONE=3; the unused CORRECT encoding need not be retained\n  as a functional state.\n\n  Expect an 8 percent fitness gain with medium confidence before final\n  evaluation. The intended gain comes from two digit selections between\n  feedback registers and moving correction to the existing EX/MEM\n  register. Input normalization plus the small head divider and 3D\n  preparation, and remaining branch/multiply paths, are timing risks.\n  Run unchanged unit and formal gates, real and ALTOPS arithmetic tests,\n  stalled reference cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/\n  marker validation. Measure all three Gowin placement options and actual\n  bracketed cycles; acceptance requires more than 135.25826 iterations/s.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/divider.sv\n    description: |\n      Compute two small leading radix-4 digits and full-width registered\n      3D at request capture. Reuse two full-width digit stages for seven\n      clocks, then expose corrected stable state in held DONE. Remove\n      the separate correction/result-register stage while preserving\n      edge-9 transfer, widths, RV32M exceptions and real/ALTOPS scheduling.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep arithmetic and handshake unchanged; update comments to explain\n      that the divider holds final state and EX/MEM registers its corrected\n      result. Preserve the base and multiply paths and ALTOPS constants.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Preserve execution control and forwarding. Update completion\n      documentation to identify EX/MEM as the correction register with\n      unchanged earliest edge-9 transfer; add no acceptance bubble.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all existing vectors and exact edge-8 result-valid/edge-9\n      transfer assertions in real and ALTOPS builds. Add exhaustive head\n      nibble/divisor-low-nibble combinations, D=15/16 boundaries, high\n      nonzero divisor bits, 3D width overflow, signed magnitude extremes,\n      and final quotient digit patterns. Check held completion with live\n      inputs changing, reset cancellation and consecutive operations.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all current timing and architectural assertions. Extend\n      divide-after-load and consecutive-divide cases with head-network\n      boundary operands, forwarded negative inputs and independent bus\n      stalls. Check source RVFI values, bit-exact results, once-only\n      retirement, contiguous order, no repeated stores and bounded progress.\n''')\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Latency-preserving hypothesis written and schema validated')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
