{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_85", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r5s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261001-003-r5s2\ntitle: \"Compact folded-PC branch prediction with entry-local training\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion already predicts direct BRANCH/JAL and isolates\n  recovery from decode. The prior predictor experiments consistently\n  reduced CoreMark cycles from 4,491,485 to 4,201,709, and r4s2 achieved\n  113.55 iterations/sec at 47.71 MHz. Its 64 counters share direction\n  state between branches only 256 bytes apart. The predictor version\n  occupies 3,630 LUT4 and 794 FF, versus 3,257 LUT4 and 664 FF for the\n  prior no-predictor recovery-cleanup candidate, whose median clock was\n  49.963 MHz. That comparison includes placement effects and does not\n  establish that the predictor lies on the critical path.\n\n  A read-only architectural CoreMark trace contains 529,588 conditional\n  branches within the timing markers. Sequential two-bit predictor\n  replay estimates 51,854 misses for the current 64-entry PC[7:2] table,\n  39,812 for a 512-entry table, and 50,577 for a 32-entry table indexed\n  by PC[6:2] XOR PC[11:7]. Replay is not a cycle-accurate simulation of\n  simultaneous fetch and EX training. Nevertheless, it shows that\n  enlarging direction state offers too little cycle benefit to clear\n  the acceptance threshold. The stronger angle is retaining accuracy\n  with half as many counters and substantially simpler update wiring.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the 64-entry bimodal table with 32 two-bit saturating counters\n  indexed by PC[6:2] XOR PC[11:7], initialized to weakly taken. This is\n  one compact conditional-direction predictor architecture. Fold the\n  next five instruction-address bits into the low five, rather than\n  merely truncating the old index, to reduce regular code-layout aliasing.\n  Use the identical five-bit hash for fetch lookup and EX training.\n  Retain the existing direct\n  BRANCH/JAL target generation, unpredicted JALR, radix-4 divider,\n  recovery at the ID/EX boundary, and pipeline depth.\n\n  Implement training as 32 independent constant-index counter registers\n \n…[3285 of 6357 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6983911b7b2620b8]…\ne bits and drive\n      in.pc[6:2] XOR in.pc[11:7]. Preserve EX-acceptance qualification,\n      conditional-only training, and all recovery, trap, and RVFI behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Narrow only the internal conditional-branch training index wiring.\n      Keep all external ports and the single-retirement contract intact.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update the independent predictor model to 32 counters and the\n      folded hash. Relocate alias-dependent stimuli to deliberate new\n      collisions rather than removing recovery assertions. Exercise\n      old-index collisions separated by the hash, actual new-index\n      collisions, all saturating transitions, back-to-back branches\n      reading pre-edge state, and EX holds without duplicate training.\n      Preserve warm-loop and exit checks, instruction/data stall tests,\n      wrong-path memory/divide protection, legality and alignment\n      checks, full retirement PC sequences, and contiguous RVFI order.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
