{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_49", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r6s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261001-003-r6s2',\n 'title':'Local counter updates remove the predictor training read mux',\n 'category':'predictor',\n 'motivation':'''The champion already uses a useful 64-entry bimodal BRANCH/JAL predictor,\nwith 4,201,709 bracketed CoreMark cycles and 54.331 MHz median Fmax.\nThe r5s2 investigation estimated only 51,854 conditional direction misses,\nso larger prediction tables alone offer little cycle headroom. Its\nreset-independent implementation showed that reducing predictor logic can\nimprove whole-core timing. The current array uses a variable-indexed\nread-modify-write counter update, potentially creating a second 64-way\nread mux, shared saturation arithmetic, and broadcast write data alongside\nthe fetch lookup. Local updates can eliminate that unnecessary network\nwithout changing prediction accuracy, reset semantics, or recovery latency.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Keep the existing 64 two-bit counters and their PC[7:2] indexing, but\nimplement training as 64 generated per-entry sequential processes.\nEntry i resets synchronously to 2'b10. Otherwise it updates only when\ntrain_valid && train_index == i, using only its own old two-bit state and\nthe broadcast train_taken bit. Saturate toward 11 for taken and 00 for\nnot taken exactly as the current implementation does. Use a width-correct\nsix-bit constant for each generated write-address comparison. Preserve the\nsame combinational fetch read of counters[pc[7:2]][1]. This should replace\nthe dynamic training read and global write-data distribution with a write\naddress decoder and small local next-state logic.\n\nThis is one predictor implementation change. Preserve table capacity,\nbinary state encoding, weakly-taken reset, every branch/JAL prediction,\ndirect-target alignment, pre-edge lookup on simultaneous read/write, and\none-time EX training of accepted legal conditional branches. Local logic\nmust update the current entry rather than a captured stale prediction.\nHeld branches, bubbles, jumps, traps, and reset do not train. Preserve\nunpredicted JALR, immediate EX correction and its priority over imem stalls,\nraw fetch/decode payloads, zeroed invalid ID/EX controls, the dedicated\nmemory effective address, divider latency, nret=1, and all external ports.\nDo not combine this with reset-independent counters, additional history,\na larger table, return prediction, delayed recovery, or arithmetic changes.\n\nThe expectation is an 8 percent net fitness gain from reduced predictor\nlogic and routing at identical CoreMark cycles. This is an unmeasured\nestimate; FPGA synthesis might already perform the transformation or\nreplicated update enables may erase the benefit. An isolated timing\nexperiment must show a real median Fmax gain, not merely an RTL rewrite.\nAt unchanged cycles, acceptance requires more than approximately\n56.83 MHz and 135.25826 iterations/s. Use actual bracketed cycles and\nmedian Gowin placement results to calculate the final fitness.\n\nRun existing pipeline tests, all unchanged formal checks, and stalled\nreference cosimulation. Check state reset, saturation in both directions,\naliasing, simultaneous lookup/training, and exactly-once accepted updates.\nRetain all CRC, UART, timing-marker, legality, alignment, memory, and RVFI\nassertions. CoreMark should retain 4,201,709 bracketed cycles; any difference\nrequires explaining and checking the predictor transition equivalence.\n''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Replace the single variable-indexed counter read-modify-write process\nwith 64 generated per-entry clocked processes. Keep counters[0:63],\ntwo-bit saturation, synchronous weakly-taken reset, and pre-edge lookup.\nEach entry has a constant six-bit comparison to train_index and locally\ncomputes its next state. Leave fetch prediction, PC control, target\narithmetic, and every module interface unchanged.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve existing pipeline and prediction assertions. Add a focused\ntraining sequence that exercises all 64 rows, saturates each direction,\ninterleaves same-index opposite-outcome branches, and holds a resolving\nbranch behind dmem backpressure. Compare observed predictions with the\noriginal two-bit state machine and assert exactly-once training, correct\nreset, unchanged recovery timing, and absence of wrong-path side effects.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=85))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated hypothesis; schema valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
