{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_79", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r6s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261001-003-r6s2\ntitle: \\\"Retain bimodal history across reset on the current champion\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 129.31 iterations/s at 54.331 MHz and\n  4,201,709 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its 64-entry bimodal predictor\n  already eliminates most useful direct-control bubbles. Trace replay\n  estimates 51,854 conditional misses in the measured region; increasing\n  the table to 512 entries removes only about 12,000 of them, too few\n  cycles to justify a larger lookup network by accuracy alone.\n\n  LESSONS.md and log.jsonl show that r5s2 removed predictor reset logic and\n  improved its older parent by 5.78 percent, but lost that round to the\n  separate memory effective-address path. Apply that single predictor\n  change to the current champion while retaining its effective_addr path.\n  A fresh isolated prototype measures 56.862 MHz median and unchanged\n  CoreMark cycles, giving 135.33065 iterations/s, approximately 4.656\n  percent above the current champion. This narrowly clears the required\n  4.6 percent; it is preliminary prototype evidence, not an orchestrator\n  acceptance result.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retain the existing bimodal direction counters across runtime reset.\n  Keep the array as 64 two-bit saturating counters, indexed by PC[7:2],\n  and preserve its binary state encoding and weakly-taken startup value.\n  Add a synthesizable initial block with a loop assigning counters[i]\n  to 2'b10 for i=0..63. Replace the counter process's synchronous reset\n  arm with an update enable of \"'!reset && train_valid. Leave the existing\n  variable-indexed saturating increment/decrement body unchanged. During\n  runtime reset, every counter holds its current value. Gowin must carry\n  the initial values into configuration, and simulation/formal must use\n  the same initial state.\n\n  Only predictor history persists. PC, register file, pipeline controls\n  and valid bits, divider state, and RVFI order retain their existing\n  reset behavior. EX still verifies every captured predicted_taken bit\n  against the actual legal, aligned transfer decision using forwarded\n  operands. A stale prediction after reset therefore changes performance\n  only: correction restores the resolved target or PC+4 and squashes the\n  younger instruction before any architectural side effect or retirement.\n  Correct operation must not depend on any particular retained history.\n\n  Preserve pre-edge lookup when fetch and training use the same index,\n  updating the current counter exactly once for an accepted legal\n  conditional branch without an actual target-alignment fault. Retain\n  direct BRANCH/JAL prediction, unpredicted JALR, full-width targets,\n  decoder legality, trap handling, imem/dmem backpressure, immediate EX\n  recovery priority, raw fetch/decode payloads, and invalid-bubble clearing.\n  Preserve the dedicated memory address path, forwarding retention,\n  divide handshakes and ALTOPS, pipeline depth, nret=1 and external ports.\n  No table resizing, local-update rewrite, history hashing, return stack,\n  additional pipeline stage, RAM primitive, or arithmetic change belongs\n  in this candidate.\n\n  The prototype used exactly this counter-reset change on the supplied\n  champion. Unchanged vendor builds for placement options 0/1/2 measured\n  52.998/56.862/56.862 MHz. Placement-0 logic fell from 3,772 to 3,490;\n  registers remained 826, LUT-RAM 8, BSRAM 6, and DSP 6. Thus the observed\n  benefit is reduced reset-related logic and physical implementation,\n  not conversion of the predictor into RAM. Do not claim that prediction\n  accuracy improved or that the predictor was the measured worst path.\n\n  The prototype passed all 53 unchanged single-channel formal checks in\n  ALTOPS mode. Reference cosimulation passed selftest and all three random\n  programs, each with and without bus stalls. Canonical stalled CoreMark\n  reached EBREAK with oob=false, all five expected CRCs, the validation\n  literal, and markers at cycles 15,053 and 4,216,762: exactly 4,201,709\n  measured cycles. Combining these cycles with median timing yields\n  135.33065 iterations/s. Acceptance requires more than 135.25826, about\n  56.832 MHz at unchanged cycles. The small margin makes final vendor\n  timing essential; the estimated five-percent gain is rounded, not a\n  claim of a large acceptance margin.\n\n  Adapt predictor tests to distinguish configuration initialization from\n  runtime reset. Establish counter preconditions through architectural\n  training or a fresh simulator instance. Retain a reference history model\n  across CPU resets instead of assuming every reset recreates weakly-taken\n  counters. Preserve all architectural assertions, exact saturation,\n  pre-edge lookup, and once-only training checks. Add trained-history reset\n  cases that force wrong predictions in both directions, including aliases,\n  stalls and poison stores/divides on the wrong path. Verify architectural\n  reset and RVFI order restart while predictor history survives, and no\n  training occurs while reset is asserted. Run the completed unit suite,\n  unchanged formal and cosimulation gates, and canonical CoreMark/vendor\n  evaluation on the final implementation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Initialize counters[0:63] to 2'\"'b10 in a synthesizable initial loop.\n      Remove only their synchronous reset assignments and guard the\n      existing indexed saturation process with \"'!reset && train_valid.\n      Retain predictor interfaces, names, state encoding, lookup, training,\n      PC reset, target calculation and recovery logic. Document the\n      distinction between configuration initialization and runtime reset.\n      Preserve the measured implementation structure without assuming RAM\n      inference or rewriting the counter update network.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing architectural checks and adapt predictor fixtures\n      for history retention across CPU resets. Use explicit training or\n      fresh simulations for tests requiring known counter values. Maintain\n      the reference counter state across runtime reset and retain exact\n      saturation, aliasing, pre-edge lookup and once-only training checks.\n      Add repeated-reset tests with trained entries, reset during a would-be\n      training event, both recovery directions, independent bus stalls and\n      wrong-path memory/divide instructions. Assert architectural-state and\n      RVFI-order restart, retained predictor history, no reset-time training,\n      and no stale retirement or wrong-path side effects.\n'\"''')\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\ns=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(s,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert s['id']=='hyp-20261001-003-r6s2' and s['category']=='predictor'\nassert type(s['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Final hypothesis schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
