{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_30", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r10s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Use a balanced monotonic-fit selection tree in the radix-8 divider'\nd['motivation']='''The champion reaches 161.21 iterations/s at 64.119 MHz. Its worst reported path traverses both radix-8 trial-subtract/select stages. Earlier byte carry-select and new grouped-borrow-prefix probes regressed, as did moving more dividend bits into preparation. The existing adjacent-fit one-hot encoding adds logic before masking and OR-reducing the candidate remainders. Monotonic trial fits permit a balanced decision tree using the original fit signals directly, without changing arithmetic or divide latency.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Replace only the remainder and quotient digit selection in each of the two existing radix-8 restoring stages. Keep all seven native full-width trial subtractors and the original 35-bit shifted values and divisor multiples. Define fit[k]=\"'!trial[k][35] as before. Because the positive multiples are ordered, select the largest fitting digit through a balanced three-level binary tree:\n  upper pair 6/7 uses fit[7]; pair 4/5 uses fit[5]; choose between them using fit[6].\n  lower pair 2/3 uses fit[3]; pair 0/1 uses fit[1]; choose between them using fit[2].\n  choose upper versus lower half using fit[4].\nThe candidate for digit zero is shifted[31:0]; all other candidates are trial[k][31:0]. Express these as a nested balanced mux tree rather than a linear priority chain or encoded digit followed by an indexed mux. Drive quotient digit bit 2 from fit[4], bit 1 from fit[4]?fit[6]:fit[2], and bit 0 from fit[4]?(fit[6]?fit[7]:fit[5]):(fit[2]?fit[3]:fit[1]). This directly exploits monotonicity and removes the adjacent-fit take[k] qualification and masked eight-way OR. For D=0 all fits are true, so digit seven wins and the existing final zero-divisor override remains correct.\n\nPreserve the original two-bit preparation peel and 2+5*6=32 bit schedule, request/normalization edge 1, preparation edge 2, five iterations edges 3..7, correction edge 8 and earliest consumption edge 9. Preserve every trial arithmetic width, divisor multiple, state, reset and handshake, stable completion, original saved operands, signed correction and RV32M exceptional result. Leave ALTOPS formulas and control timing identical. This is one local arithmetic selector micro-optimization; no pipeline, multiplier, predictor, memory, decoder or retirement-width change is proposed.\n\nThe intended mapping is three levels of direct two-input selection with all controls computed in parallel, instead of generating one-hot adjacent transitions then masking and reducing all candidate data. Vendor mapping can still favor the original network, and other EX paths may limit the gain. Validate direct selection equivalence for every possible monotonic fit vector and arbitrary candidate data, then real and ALTOPS ALU tests, unchanged formal and stalled reference cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks. Inspect all normal Gowin placement options and actual bracketed cycles. Target a six percent unmeasured improvement with low confidence. Acceptance requires more than 168.62566 iterations/s, approximately 67.069 MHz if 3,977,318 cycles are retained. Do not weaken tests or select only the fastest placement.'\"'''\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes'][0]['description']='Replace adjacent-fit one-hot remainder selection in both radix-8 steps with the exact balanced fit[4]/fit[2,6]/fit[1,3,5,7] mux tree, and derive quotient digits directly from the same decision tree. Keep the full-width native subtraction, preparation and eight-clock protocol unchanged.'\nd['changes'][1]['description']='Keep every existing arithmetic, latency, reset and held-result assertion. Add directed quotient-digit 0..7 and boundary cases for both radix-8 steps, high 35-bit divisor multiples, D=0 and all signed quadrants. Validate the selector for all nine monotonic-fit possibilities including all-false and all-true, with arbitrary candidate words, against largest-fitting-digit selection.'\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=86));jsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated schema-valid selector hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
