ain outweigh blocking divide latency in CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r2s0 (improvement, +27.81%): Reducing the restoring divider from eight to six steps per clock shortens the critical remainder_q feedback chain enough to outweigh two extra blocking cycles in CoreMark, with a final step[2] selection needed to process exactly 32 bits.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 (regression, +9.50%): Suppressing correct BRANCH/JAL r\n…[6418 of 9490 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b6afd80399d3ff28]…\nct arbitration, so median Fmax falling from 79.422 to 71.484 MHz outweighs the bubble savings.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r13s0 (improvement, +6.25%): Predecoding ID/EX branch predicates for a balanced comparator and capturing multiplier sign/half-select bits with operands jointly shorten competing EX/DSP paths without adding CoreMark cycles, clearing the acceptance gate that neither isolated predecode reached.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r13s1 (regression, -36.79%): Registering operand routes does not isolate control timing when branch comparison shares the candidate-slice carry tree: alu_op-to-IF recovery became critical, dropping median Fmax from 79.422 to 50.205 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r13s2 (regression, +5.32%): Moving branch forwarding selection to one-bit results of seven balanced comparators and predecoding mismatch polarity removes mux-before-compare delay at unchanged CoreMark cycles, but its +5.32% gain trails joint branch/multiplier predecode (+6.25%).\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
