{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_78","type":"agent_message","text":"The branch bypass reduced CoreMark cycles by 3.26%, passed all pipeline tests, and passed standard cosimulation. Its first timing result nearly matched the champion’s fitness but did not clear acceptance. I’m checking one final refinement: registering the non-load forwarding qualifier with EX/MEM to avoid adding a gate to the ALU forwarding path.\n"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_79", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r7s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-001-r7s0',\n 'title':'Bypass completed loads directly to branch comparison without an interlock bubble',\n 'category':'micro_opt',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 136.42 iterations/s at 57.319 MHz and 4,201,709 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior micro_opt wins removed unnecessary dependencies without adding execution latency. Fresh isolated mux, multiplier-sharing, and divider simplification experiments did not improve median timing on this parent. A branch-only load bypass instead removes a real instruction bubble while keeping memory data outside the multiplier/general ALU operand cone. A prototype reduced CoreMark to 4,064,795 cycles, a 3.26 percent cycle reduction, and passed canonical validation and reference cosimulation. This makes load-to-branch interlocking the most promising tested throughput opportunity.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Add a MEM-to-EX bypass exclusively for conditional-branch comparison and eliminate the load-use bubble only when the fetched instruction is a legal conditional branch. Preserve the existing pipeline stages, retirement width, prediction policy, operation latencies, and both memory handshakes. Do not bypass loads into the general ALU or multiplier, and do not remove interlocks for arithmetic, stores, or JALR.\n\nIn mem_stage expose the existing sign/zero-extended load_data and a branch_load_valid qualifier equal to in.valid && in.ctrl.mem_read && ctrl_with_trap.reg_write. Thus LB/LBU/LH/LHU/LW use their exact architectural values and misaligned loads cannot advertise a result. Route these signals and the EX/MEM destination tag to ex_stage. For each branch comparison operand, select branch_load_data when branch_load_valid, destination is nonzero, and its tag matches that source; otherwise select the existing forwarded rs value. Use these two new values only in all six conditional-branch comparisons and the branch's captured RVFI source values. Leave alu_a, alu_b, effective-address generation, store data, divider launch values, and jump-target generation on their existing operands.\n\nPrevent a load's effective address from masquerading as register data on the ordinary EX/MEM forwarding path. Add a single forward_alu_valid bit to ex_mem_t. Capture ctrl_with_trap.reg_write && \"'!ctrl_with_trap.mem_read alongside the accepted EX instruction, clear it with existing reset/bubble clears, and retain it through data-memory holds. Use this REGISTERED bit as forward_unit'\"'s EX/MEM write eligibility. Retain the existing destination-zero check, tag comparison, EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority, and MEM/WB forwarding without valid gating. In particular, a faulting load must leave a dependent branch reading the prior architectural value via retained WB or ID/EX data; forwarding its address is incorrect. Registering eligibility avoids inserting a new mem_read gate on the timed EX forwarding path.\n\nIn hazard_unit qualify the load-use interlock with \"'!if_id_branch. Compute if_id_branch directly from the raw fetch word: opcode 0x63 and funct3 neither 2 nor 3. Keep imem_ready, valid, nonzero destination, source comparisons, and every other hazard term. A stalled older memory operation still freezes EX/MEM and the dependent branch in ID/EX. EX'\"'s existing ex_accept already forbids redirect, training, or branch transfer until that memory operation can advance. On the ready edge the load enters MEM/WB and the branch enters EX/MEM using its completed data, with normal in-order retirement. Preserve redirect priority, once-only training, misaligned target handling, wrong-path squash, and all decoder legality defaults.\n\nThis is one local forwarding/interlock optimization, not a new predictor or memory architecture. All addresses remain full width; retain nret=1, every top-level port, all ALTOPS and real M arithmetic, division completion timing, contiguous RVFI order, canonical CoreMark checks, and all unchanged formal gates.\n\nPreliminary evidence for the branch-only bypass with combinational non-load forwarding qualification: all 26 existing pipeline tests passed, the standard cosimulation evaluator passed five ELFs including stalled CoreMark validation, and the benchmark completed in 4,064,795 bracketed cycles. Gowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 54.665/55.350/55.350 MHz, giving approximately 136.169 iterations/s, below the champion and below acceptance. That prototype establishes the cycle benefit, NOT a fitness improvement. The final proposal registers the forwarding qualifier as described above to protect the general ALU path. Its timing must be measured separately. Expected gain is deliberately low-confidence because divider feedback and physical routing may still dominate.\n\nRetain all existing tests and add directed load-to-branch tests with dependencies on either or both sources, every load width/sign mode, all six branch predicates, x0, youngest-writer priority, and misaligned loads following an older writer of the same register. Exercise independent long bus stalls, ready-edge release, mispredictions, reset, and wrong-path/invalid fetches. Check exact RVFI source data, PCs, order, trap handling, stable outstanding memory requests, and once-only retirement/training. Add cycle assertions proving the no-wait load-to-branch pair loses its former bubble while ALU/store/JALR consumers still interlock. Run the unchanged full formal and cosimulation gates; preliminary simulation is not a formal proof. Acceptance requires measured fitness above 142.69532 iterations/s, approximately 58.005 MHz at the prototype cycle count, using the actual final cycles and median Fmax.''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add forward_alu_valid to ex_mem_t as a registered non-load forwarding qualifier; retain all other bundle fields and interfaces.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Accept completed load data/valid/tag and select separate branch-only comparison operands. Capture those operands for branch RVFI. Register non-load forwarding eligibility with existing EX/MEM reset/bubble/hold behavior. Preserve all general ALU operands, arithmetic units, effective addresses, and execution handshakes.'},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Expose existing extended load_data and valid, nontrapping load write eligibility for branch comparison. Preserve memory ports, alignment traps, byte extraction, MEM/WB capture, and retained forwarding data.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire the branch load bypass, use registered EX/MEM forward_alu_valid for ordinary forwarding, and pass a validated raw conditional-branch predicate to the hazard unit. Keep external IO and RVFI shape unchanged.'},\n {'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Suppress only the load-use bubble for a validated conditional branch. Retain data/instruction bus backpressure, divide holds, redirect recovery, and all nonbranch load-use interlocks.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain all assertions and add branch-load bypass, signed/unsigned subword, both-source, x0, faulting-load fallback, prior-writer, reset, stalled-release, misprediction, and once-only retirement tests. Verify the intended cycle reduction and continued interlocks for ALU/store/JALR consumers.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Written and schema validated:',p)\nprint('Required Fmax:',142.69532*4064795/10000000)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
