{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 (improvement, +481.85%): Splitting shared DIV/REM into four clocked eight-step restoring iterations with separately registered normalization and correction breaks the full-width combinational EX dependency, making the global clock gain outweigh blocking divide latency in CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r2s0 (improvement, +27.81%): Reducing the restoring divider from eight to six steps per clock shortens the critical remainder_q feedback chain enough to outweigh two extra blocking cycles in CoreMark, with a final step[2] selection needed to process exactly 32 bits.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 (regression, +9.50%): Suppressing correct BRANCH/JAL redirects with a 64-entry bimodal predictor improved the eight-step divider parent, but lost to the six-step divider's larger clock gain; a regression against a different divider does not imply prediction is harmful.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r3s1 (improvement, +12.46%): Peeling the first radix-4 digit lets 3D be registered outside the feedback chain without adding a cycle, enabling three radix-4 stages with parallel D/2D/3D trials to replace six serial binary stages and improve CoreMark fitness at unchanged divide latency.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r4s1 (regression, +9.35%): Removing the redirect-driven IF NOP mux breaks the EX-to-decode/register-read dependency, but independently capturing ID/EX payload on flush showed no additional fitness benefit over the simpler fetch-mux removal (110.84 versus 111.24 iterations/s).\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r4s2 (improvement, +12.03%): On the radix-4 core, 64-entry bimodal BRANCH/JAL prediction and recovery at the ID/EX boundary combine fetch-bubble reduction with removal of the EX-redirect-to-decode/load-use dependency, improving CoreMark fitness without changing divider latency.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r5s0 (improvement, +13.88%): Registering the full forwarded rs1 + immediate sum in EX/MEM.effective_addr isolates RAM addressing and byte-lane logic from the multiplier/general ALU result mux, raising median Fmax to 54.331 MHz without changing CoreMark's 4,201,709 cycles.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r5s1 (regression, +13.41%): Sharing MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU behind operand/result registers raises Fmax, but two extra EX wait cycles per multiply add 4.46% CoreMark cycles, leaving it slower than isolating EX/MEM.effective_addr without added stalls (128.78 versus 129.31 iterations/s).\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r5s2 (regression, +5.78%): Initializing bimodal counters in an initial loop and retaining them across runtime reset removes reset logic and improves Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles, but yields a smaller clock gain than isolating EX/MEM.effective_addr (120.11 versus 129.31 iterations/s).\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261001-001-r6s0 (improvement, +5.50%): Replacing bulk register-file data reset with a written bitmap, distributed RAM attribution and mutually exclusive masked bypass/stored reads restores explicit ID/EX operand registers, removing the BSRAM-to-EX launch path and improving Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r7s0 (regression, -8.05%): Restricting MEM-to-EX load forwarding to branch comparisons does not preserve Fmax by itself: the prototype's 3.26% CoreMark cycle reduction was outweighed by its drop from 57.319 to 55.350 MHz.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r7s1 (improvement, +5.07%): A four-entry circular fall-through instruction queue improves CoreMark by using load-use, divide and data-memory holds to prefetch, then feeding decode during instruction-bus stalls without adding latency when the queue is empty.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r7s2 (regression, -4.41%): Carrying IF direct_target and PC+4 through 64 extra ID/EX bits removes EX adders but leaves divider remainder_q feedback critical, so unchanged CoreMark cycles cannot offset median Fmax falling from 57.319 to 54.790 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
