{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_97", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r8s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-001-r8s0\ntitle: \"Decouple radix-4 digit selection from full-width subtraction carry\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 143.34 iterations/s at 57.009 MHz and\n  3,977,318 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path crosses\n  105 cells from divider quotient_q to remainder_q. Each of three\n  serial radix-4 steps waits for full-width subtraction borrow before\n  selecting the next remainder. This feedback limits every instruction.\n  Prior micro_opt hyp-20261001-001-r3s0 replaced subtraction with byte\n  carry-select arithmetic and regressed to 24.392 MHz; duplicating byte\n  differences and their selection is therefore poor evidence for a win.\n  This proposal instead preserves native subtraction for result data\n  and gives the digit decision its own balanced magnitude-comparison tree.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change only the combinational recurrence in rtl/divider.sv. For each\n  existing radix-4 step form the same 34-bit T={R,next_two_dividend_bits}\n  and use the same full-width D, 2D and registered 3D. Determine T>=D,\n  T>=2D and T>=3D with three parallel explicit unsigned lexicographic\n  comparison trees, independently of the trial-difference carry outputs.\n\n  Implement a portable local 34-bit comparison helper using eight\n  four-bit groups plus a two-bit most-significant group. For each group\n  compute greater-than and equality. Combine adjacent high/low groups\n  with GT=GT_high | (EQ_high & GT_low), EQ=EQ_high & EQ_low, in a\n  balanced tree, padding to sixteen leaves with equal zero groups at\n  the most-significant end if helpful. Return GE=GT | EQ. Use explicit\n  static levels rather than a serial priority loop or a single wide\n  relational expression that can map back into the old carry chain.\n  Preserve all 34 bits in comparison, especially the high bits of 2D\n  and 3D; truncating those comparisons is incorrect for large divisors.\n\n  In parallel, compute each candidate remainder with ordinary unsigned\n  32-bit modular subtraction T[31:0]-multiple[31:0]. These are exactl\n…[2416 of 5488 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d093bdba4f3c8543]…\nycles, and inspect\n  the mapped critical path to establish whether digit selection improved.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/divider.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the three radix-4 stages' borrow-driven digit decisions\n      with parallel balanced 34-bit lexicographic comparison helpers.\n      Keep native 32-bit low-result subtractors, the existing masked\n      remainder mux, peeled first digit, full-width divisor multiples,\n      state machine, request/result handshake and ALTOPS behavior.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all existing arithmetic and latency checks. Add directed\n      and seeded random DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases stressing nibble-boundary\n      comparisons, exact multiple boundaries, large divisor multiples,\n      signed extremes and exceptional results. Retain reset and held\n      completion checks; verify independent arithmetic equivalence of\n      the comparison helper and radix-4 step during implementation.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
