{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_52", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r9s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261002-001-r9s0\ntitle: \\\"Share all combinational multiply variants through one signed 33-bit product\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 153.70 iterations/s at 61.131 MHz and 3,977,318\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst path traverses WB forwarding,\n  EX and the ALU. The current ALU independently computes signed-signed,\n  unsigned-unsigned and signed-unsigned 64-bit products; Gowin allocates\n  six DSPs. This duplicates multiplier input fanout and output selection\n  on an already critical operand-to-result path. The prior clocked shared\n  multiplier reduced DSPs to two but added two cycles per multiply and\n  4.46 percent CoreMark cycles. Sharing the arithmetic while retaining\n  combinational execution isolates its area/routing benefit without that\n  cycle penalty. A preliminary WB-selector-only retiming trial reached\n  only 62.052 MHz median, below the acceptance margin, and exposed a\n  critical path through the unsigned multiplier. That retiming is not\n  part of this hypothesis.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the three real combinational products in alu.sv with one\n  signed 33-by-33-bit multiplier and a signed 66-bit product. Form\n    mul_a = \"'$signed({a_signed && a[31], a});\n    mul_b = $signed({b_signed && b[31], b});\n  where a_signed is true for MULH or MULHSU and b_signed is true only\n  for MULH. Thus MUL and MULHU use zero-extended operands, MULH uses\n  two sign-extended operands, and MULHSU sign-extends only A. Both\n  multiplication operands must be explicitly signed and exactly 33 bits;\n  the full product must be 66 bits so expression sizing cannot discard\n  high result bits. One 33-bit signed value represents every unsigned\n  32-bit input as a nonnegative number, including 0xffffffff.\n\n  Select product[31:0] for MUL and product[63:32] for MULH, MULHU and\n  MULHSU. Ignore only bits 65:64. The high-result variants share the same\n  output slice because input extension encodes their signedness. Do not\n  add post-product sign-correction subtractors, a second product for\n  low-half multiplication, or a registered request/completion interface.\n  Eliminate all three old multiplication expressions. Keep this one\n  product combinational behind the current ALU interface and preserve\n  the existing single-cycle EX acceptance, forwarding and stall behavior.\n  This is one arithmetic-resource-sharing micro-optimization, with no\n  pipeline, WB, register-file, predictor or divider redesign.\n\n  Preserve every RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formula and its combinational\n  multiply timing exactly. The formal substitutions continue to use\n  original 32-bit a and b; the real multiplier must not enter their\n  arithmetic cone. Preserve divider state, latency and handshakes,\n  decoder legality, traps, all RVFI source/result data, nret=1, retirement\n  ordering, bus backpressure and every contract check. No specialization\n  to CoreMark operands or opcode frequency is permitted.\n\n  Sharing should reduce the physical design from three MULT36X36\n  operators to one, or six to two reported DSPs, reducing operand fanout,\n  routing pressure and result mux complexity while leaving CoreMark\n  cycles unchanged. Inspect Gowin synthesis and post-route paths to\n  confirm that sharing occurred. Operation-dependent extension bits may\n  create a new multiplier-control path, and the competing divider can\n  limit the gain; lower resource use alone is not evidence of fitness.\n\n  Keep existing arithmetic and pipeline tests. Exercise the same operand\n  pair under all four multiplication opcodes back-to-back, especially\n  0xffffffff, 0x80000000, 0x7fffffff, zero, one and powers of two with\n  adjacent values. Compare low and high products to exact RV32M integer\n  references, including signed-by-unsigned asymmetry. Exercise forwarded\n  operands and dependent consumers with instruction/data stalls. Run\n  unchanged real/ALTOPS ALU tests, pipeline tests, formal and reference\n  cosimulation, plus canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks.\n  Measure all normal Gowin placement options and actual bracketed cycles.\n  Acceptance requires fitness above 160.7702 iterations/s, or median\n  Fmax above 63.9435 MHz if cycles remain 3,977,318. The expected gain is\n  a prediction until this prototype'\"'s median timing is available.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace mul_ss, mul_uu and mul_su with explicitly signed 33-bit\n      operands and one 66-bit combinational product. Select extension\n      signs by multiply opcode, return bits 31:0 or 63:32, and remove\n      duplicate multiply expressions. Preserve ports, timing, division,\n      all base operations and every ALTOPS formula.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing vectors and latency assertions. Extend real\n      arithmetic coverage by cycling all four multiply opcodes on each\n      extreme or deterministic random operand pair, including unequal\n      signed/unsigned operands, then changing inputs without a clock\n      edge. Check exact low/high products and retain the existing ALTOPS\n      reference behavior.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing coverage and add mixed MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU\n      sequences using both forwarding sources, same-rd writers and\n      immediate consumers under imem/dmem holds. Check architectural\n      source/result values, unchanged execute timing and once-only\n      ordered retirement.\n''')\nprint('Revised hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
