{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_63","type":"agent_message","text":"Multiplier sharing reduced DSP use but reached only 61.437 MHz median. I’m testing one final, narrower change: removing bubble-clearing logic from the EX/MEM ALU-result register. Invalid entries will still have zeroed controls and cannot forward or retire; their unused result data can capture normally, shortening the timed result path."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_64", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r9s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261002-001-r9s0\ntitle: \\\"Remove bubble selection from the EX/MEM arithmetic-result register\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 153.70 iterations/s at 61.131 MHz with 3,977,318\n  CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path ends at EX/MEM.alu_result\n  after WB forwarding and the ALU. In ex_stage, reset, data-memory hold,\n  execute_wait and invalid-instruction handling currently control the\n  whole EX/MEM bundle, so even the 32-bit arithmetic result is selected\n  to zero when inserting an invalid bubble. Forwarding and architectural\n  side effects are already suppressed by the bubble's zero controls.\n  Removing that unnecessary result-data selection can shorten the timed\n  ALU-to-register path without changing any instruction latency. Prior\n  input-mux flattening regressed, while the effective-address-register\n  separation showed that isolating timed data from unnecessary selection\n  can improve fitness. This proposal targets the result-register input.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change only EX/MEM.alu_result's behavior on the existing execute_wait\n  or \"'!in.valid bubble arm in ex_stage. Retain the current reset-first,\n  data-memory-stall-second priority. On reset clear the entire bundle.\n  On stall retain the entire bundle and the outstanding memory request.\n  On an unstalled bubble continue clearing the entire bundle to zero,\n  but override alu_result with the same candidate captured for a real\n  instruction: in.ctrl.is_jump ? (in.pc + 32'\"'d4) : alu_result.\n  In SystemVerilog this can be expressed as the existing reg_q <= '0\n  followed by reg_q.alu_result <= candidate within the same bubble arm,\n  with both assignments owned by the current single always_ff process.\n  The later field assignment takes priority over the whole-bundle zero.\n  Keep the normal valid-instruction arm unchanged.\n\n  Equivalently, the result register now clears on reset, holds on stall,\n  and otherwise captures the arithmetic/link candidate regardless of\n  in.valid or execute_wait. Do not hold the result on bubbles: that would\n  introduce a different enable depending on the same late controls.\n  The desired circuit has no execute_wait/in.valid selection of zero\n  on the result D input. All other bundle fields retain their existing\n  behavior, especially ctrl='0 and valid=0 on every bubble. Only the\n  unobservable arithmetic payload of an invalid entry changes.\n\n  It is essential to clear all controls, including reg_write, mem_read,\n  and mem_write. Forwarding eligibility uses reg_write independently of\n  valid, and a stale arithmetic payload must never become a writer or a\n  memory request. Preserve complete EX/MEM retention during data stalls,\n  MEM/WB retained-value forwarding after valid clears, x0 behavior and\n  youngest-writer priority. Keep effective_addr, write_data, destination\n  tags and RVFI metadata on their original capture/clear rules. Do not\n  alter the ALU or divider arithmetic, ALTOPS substitutions, predictor,\n  instruction queue, hazard logic, decode legality, nret=1, external\n  interfaces, retirement order or any contract check.\n\n  This is one local register-input control optimization, with no new\n  stage, bypass path, arithmetic implementation or cycle-count change.\n  Removing a mux after the expensive ALU/multiplier and its control\n  fanout should improve Fmax for every instruction. Check actual Gowin\n  paths to confirm that the bubble gate disappears instead of merely\n  being moved into another data-dependent enable. The competing divider\n  and forwarding paths may limit the gain. Preserve reset and data-stall\n  gating; removing required hold behavior is not part of the proposal.\n\n  Run unchanged pipeline tests, formal and stalled reference cosimulation,\n  plus canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/completion/marker checks.\n  Specifically exercise bubbles during load-use interlocks and divide\n  waits, invalid or wrong-path arithmetic, older memory operations held\n  by dmemReady, and a younger consumer using retained MEM/WB data. Assert\n  that invalid results never forward, write registers or memory, train\n  prediction or retire. Verify all real instruction result and source\n  fields, once-only retirement and unchanged execution timing.\n  Measure all three vendor placement options and actual bracketed cycles.\n  Acceptance requires fitness above 160.7702 iterations/s, approximately\n  63.9435 MHz if cycles remain 3,977,318. The gain below is a prediction\n  pending this prototype's vendor timing.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      In the execute_wait/invalid bubble arm, retain the whole-bundle\n      clear and override only reg_q.alu_result with the normal ALU/link\n      candidate. Keep reset clearing, data-stall retention, every bubble\n      control and all valid-instruction captures unchanged. Ensure the\n      synthesized result register captures on every nonreset unstalled\n      edge without an execute_wait/in.valid data mux.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing tests and exercise load-use and divide bubbles\n      with changing arithmetic candidates, wrong-path instructions,\n      same-rd writers and long dmem holds. Check architectural/RVFI\n      results, no side effects from invalid EX/MEM entries, retained WB\n      forwarding, contiguous retirement order and unchanged latency.\n''')\nprint('Revised hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
