{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_83", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,260p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r10s1.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-002-r10s1\ntitle: \"Repartition M-extension execution at operand and completion registers\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 161.21 iterations/s at 64.119 MHz and 3,977,318\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path crosses 76 cells\n  inside the divider, from quotient_q to remainder_q. The current main\n  recurrence performs two dependent radix-8 subtract/select stages per\n  clock. Prior structural attempts show that shortening divider feedback\n  can improve the global clock, but extra divide cycles threaten the\n  unchanged formal liveness bound. The r6s1 launch-peeling experiment\n  also warns against moving multiple generation onto forwarded inputs.\n  The r9s1 multiplier experiment exposed this same divider bottleneck.\n  A new divider-only prototype measured 64.578 MHz median with unchanged\n  cycles, exposing WB forwarding through the multiplier as the worst\n  path. Either change alone leaves the competing M-extension path\n  dominant. Address both with one execution-register partitioning change.\nhypothesis: |\n  Repartition the M-extension execution unit at its operand and completion\n  register boundaries: capture forwarded multiply operands before the\n  product, and shorten divider feedback to one radix-32 digit per clock.\n  These complementary parts address the competing paths measured in the\n  divider-only probe and the prior multiplier-only attempt; this is one\n  structural execution timing redesign, with no predictor or memory change.\n\n  For MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU add a separate request/result handshake in\n  alu.sv. On accepted request edge 1 capture the original 32-bit operands\n  and opcode and set a pending bit. While pending, hold them unchanged,\n  deassert request-ready and assert result-valid. Build one signed\n  33-by-33 product with a full 66-bit result from these saved operands.\n  Extend A with its sign only for MULH/MULHSU and B only for MULH;\n  otherwise extend with zero. Select product[31:0] for saved MUL or\n  product[63:32] for saved high-product opera\n…[9778 of 12850 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:cda33148cca8e440]…\npcode and operands,\n      held completion, reset and back-to-back or alternating requests.\n      Add deterministic randomized and directed cases exercising all 32\n      quotient digits, kD-1/kD/kD+1 boundaries, powers of 32 and neighbors,\n      wide odd multiples, maximal divisors, all signed quadrants, zero\n      divisors and overflow. Verify held result with changed live inputs,\n      reset during every phase and consecutive requests in both builds.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add multiply and radix-32 dependencies through ALU/load/WB forwarding,\n      adjacent mixed DIV/REM operations, immediate consumers and branches,\n      reset and bus stalls around launch/completion, same-rd writers, x0,\n      original multiply RVFI operands and alternating MUL/DIV. Preserve the existing\n      nine-cycle adjacent-divide interval and all liveness, operand/RVFI,\n      contiguous-order, wrong-path suppression and exactly-once memory\n      checks. Do not weaken any existing assertion or bound.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
