{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_54", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r11s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-002-r11s1',\n'title':'Terminate branch recovery at validity registers instead of payload write enables',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The current champion scores 185.62 iterations/s at 74.054 MHz and\n3,989,539 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path starts in\nregistered WB load data, crosses EX and ends at the fetch PC. After\nr10s1 shortened both M-extension paths, branch recovery has become a\nvisible timing limit. Current-parent probes likewise expose a long\nbranch-comparison-to-redirect chain ending at ID payload registers and\nfetch-queue RAM write enables; one report shows redirect fanout of 201.\nThe r4s1 lesson found no extra benefit from independent payload capture\non a much earlier divider-limited parent. The current critical endpoints\nand the subsequently added fetch FIFO provide new reasons to revisit\nthe validity boundary, without delaying recovery or changing prediction.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Separate instruction payload capture from pipeline validity at the\nexisting ID/EX and fetch-queue boundaries. Make EX recovery terminate\nat a small set of validity/occupancy registers instead of clearing wide\nID/EX payloads or suppressing writes into already-invalidated FIFO RAM.\nThis is one structural control partition, with no added pipeline stage,\nno changed recovery cycle and no altered accepted instruction schedule.\n\nIn id_stage retain the full reset initialization and the existing hold\npriority. If stall is asserted, retain the entire ID/EX bundle. On every\nother edge capture PC, instruction, immediate, register addresses,\nregister-file values, prediction and decoded controls irrespective of\nflush or incoming valid. Set the registered valid bit to\n\"'!flush && in.valid on that same edge. Flush and unavailable fetch must\nhave no combinational connection to the payload or decoded-control D\ninputs except through the existing, unchanged stall/hold decision.\n\nAt id_stage'\"'s output copy the registered bundle, but replace out.ctrl\nwith all zeros whenever the registered valid bit is zero. This local\npost-register control mask is essential: current forwarding and memory\nlogic inspect controls beyond valid, and invalid raw LOAD/STORE/DIV\nwords must remain completely inert. Do not remove this mask or merely\nclear valid while exposing stale reg_write, memory, jump or M-operation\ncontrols. Keep arbitrary invalid payload bits behind valid; they must\nnever retire or cause requests, redirects, training or writes. In\nparticular a flushed consumer's nonzero rd/source fields are harmless\nonly because its visible controls are zero and EX still emits a fully\ncleared EX/MEM bubble for \"'!in.valid or execute_wait.\n\nIn if_stage remove only redirect from the fetch payload RAM write\nenable: write entries[wr_ptr] on !reset && enqueue. Keep the exact\nexisting reset/redirect priority that clears count, rd_ptr and wr_ptr,\nand that replaces the speculative PC with redirect_target immediately.\nAn enqueue on the recovery edge may overwrite an arbitrary old slot,\nbut the simultaneous count/pointer clear invalidates every such word.\nAfter recovery, the first real enqueue overwrites the new live slot\nbefore it is counted. No stale packet may become visible. Keep the\nfall-through output, enqueue/dequeue equations, full simultaneous pop\nand push behavior, prediction packet and branch-training schedule.\nNever remove redirect from occupancy invalidation or PC recovery.\n\nKeep nret=1 and every top-level port, instruction latency, load-use\ninterlock, data-memory hold, blocking multiply/divide schedule, ALTOPS,\ntrap handling, decode default-illegal policy and RVFI ordering. Do not\nchange MEM/WB'\"'s special retained forwarding data/control when valid\nclears during a data stall. Keep existing forwarding-match registers\nand EX/MEM-over-WB priority. A data-memory or execution hold must keep\nthe complete live ID/EX instruction exactly as before; only advancing\nbubbles and flushed younger packets receive irrelevant payload data.\n\nThe intended gain is lower recovery fanout and removal of late branch\nlogic from wide register and distributed-RAM write enables. Extra local\nvalid-to-control gating may expose a different critical path or offset\nthe saving; the unchanged divider can also become limiting again.\nTarget a nine percent fitness improvement with low confidence pending\nmeasurement. Expect 3,989,539 bracketed cycles; acceptance requires\nmore than 194.15852 iterations/s, about 77.460 MHz at that cycle count.\nJudge the normal three-option median Gowin Fmax times actual CoreMark\niterations/cycle, not fanout count or a single placement result.\n\nRun existing real and ALTOPS pipeline/ALU tests, unchanged single-channel\nformal including liveness, and stalled reference cosimulation. Verify\ncanonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks and unchanged bracketed\ncycles. Inspect vendor paths to confirm recovery no longer drives wide\nID payload enables or FIFO RAM write enables. Never relax an assertion,\nformal depth, decoder legality or evaluation file. This proposal does\nnot include the tested WB-result retiming or one-hot forwarding variant.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':9,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Retain reset and stall priority, but capture all payload and decoded\ncontrols on every non-stalled edge independent of flush/in.valid.\nRegister valid as \"'!flush && in.valid. Expose an all-zero control bundle\nwhen registered valid is zero so invalid words remain inert while\nrecovery only clears one ID/EX validity bit. Document why invalid\npayloads may be nonzero and why the output control mask is mandatory.\n'\"'''},\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Remove redirect qualification only from the unreset queue payload RAM\nwrite enable. Keep \"'!reset && enqueue and retain redirect priority for\nPC, count and both pointers. Document recovery-edge writes as discarded\nstorage whose validity is determined solely by count/pointers.\n'\"'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve all existing assertions and add recovery cases with empty,\npartial, full and wrapped FIFOs, including enqueue/pop on the redirect\nedge, unavailable fetch and prolonged independent data stalls. Place\nraw loads, stores, MUL/DIV and jumps in discarded or unavailable words;\ncheck that invalid ID/EX controls are zero and that no request, training,\nregister write, side effect or retirement escapes. Verify held live\ninstructions preserve every field, and recovery exposes the target on\nthe original cycle with correct RVFI operands, PCs and contiguous order.\nDo not require invalid payload fields themselves to be zero.\n'''}]}\nclass D(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef represent(dumper,data):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',data,style='|' if '\\\\n' in data else None)\nD.add_representer(str,represent)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=D,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Revised structural hypothesis written and schema validated.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
