{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_79", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r11s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-002-r11s1',\n'title':'Repartition writeback data and forwarding priority at existing register boundaries',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion reaches 185.62 iterations/s at 74.054 MHz and 3,989,539\nCoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path starts in registered load data,\npasses through WB selection and EX, and ends at the fetch PC. Structural\nr8s1 successfully registered forwarding tag matches, but the current\ninterface still selects WB data and qualifies/prioritizes those matches\nafter the edge. Retiming the complete data/control interface can shorten\nthese recurrent paths without extra instruction or recovery cycles.\nA current-parent probe moving only WB data selection before its register\nmeasured 77.044 MHz median, about four percent gain, below acceptance.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Repartition the existing WB-to-EX feedback interface so the architectural\nWB value and fully resolved operand-source choices are both registered.\nThis is one structural register-boundary change, preserving the entire\npipeline schedule, memory protocol and recovery latency.\n\nReplace mem_wb_t.alu_result/read_data with one 32-bit result field.\nOn a normal MEM/WB advance, capture in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data :\nin.alu_result after existing load extraction/sign extension. wb_stage\nwires w_data directly from this register and retains its original\nvalid/reg_write write enable and destination. All existing WB consumers\nthen receive the same selected value through their existing connections.\nKeep raw RVFI memory data, masks, address and metadata separate. Reset\nclears the result; hold_wb retains it with rd/control while clearing\nvalid only. Do not qualify data capture or forwarding with valid or\nrd\"'!=0; preserve the original WB mux'\"'s value even for nonwriters, and\nleave write eligibility to existing control semantics.\n\nIn forward_unit move producer qualification and EX-over-WB priority\nbefore the edge, rather than registering only tag equality. Keep the\nexisting TWO-BIT selector encoding: 00 ID/EX, 01 EX/MEM, 10 MEM/WB.\nKeep ex_stage's existing case-based operand data muxes; do not substitute\na three-bit one-hot mask/OR datapath. A one-hot prototype mapped worse.\n\nCompute next consumer/producer addresses exactly as today:\nnext_rs = stall_id ? id_ex_rs : if_id_rs;\nnext_ex_rd = stall_ex_mem ? ex_mem_rd : id_ex_rd;\nnext_wb_rd = hold_mem_wb ? mem_wb_rd : ex_mem_rd.\nEX additionally exports advancing writer permission as\nin.valid && \"'!execute_wait && ctrl_with_trap.reg_write.\nMEM exports ctrl_with_trap.reg_write AFTER its memory-alignment checks,\nwithout qualifying by retirement valid. These are exact next controls,\nnot raw decode permissions. Select next_ex_w_en from held EX/MEM'\"'s\nreg_write when stall_ex_mem, otherwise EX's advancing permission.\nSelect next_wb_w_en from held MEM/WB's reg_write when hold_mem_wb,\notherwise MEM's trap-adjusted permission.\n\nFor each operand qualify the nonzero next source's equality with those\nnext writer permissions, choose EX/MEM first, then MEM/WB, then ID/EX,\nand register the final two-bit choice. Reset selects ID/EX. Refresh\nEVERY non-reset edge, including held consumers while older producers\ndrain; do not freeze choices with stall_id. No live producer qualifier\nor priority encoder remains after these registers. Forwarding from a\nretired WB result must remain available during dmem holds even though\nMEM/WB.valid has cleared. EX wait/bubble controls and trap suppression\nmust be reflected exactly in the pre-edge permissions. A flushed\nconsumer cannot act; its next replacement receives newly computed\nchoices. Preserve x0 handling and younger-writer priority.\n\nKeep nret=1, all top-level IO, decode legality, precise RVFI values and\norder, memory handshakes, load-use bubbles, multiply/divide and ALTOPS\nschedules, predictor and fetch FIFO. Do not add a pipeline stage or\ndelay branch resolution. Do not include the separately probed validity\nand FIFO-write changes, which reduced median Fmax. The cost tradeoff\nis moving the WB mux into MEM's load-result input cone and moving final\ntrap-aware source selection into the preceding cycle. Either can become\nthe new critical path; Gowin mapping and the unchanged divider also\nlimit confidence in the clock gain.\n\nThe selected-result-only probe passed both existing pipeline builds,\nstalled reference cosimulation and CoreMark validation; its normal\nplacement options were 73.181/77.044/77.044 MHz. A version that additionally\nregistered THREE-BIT one-hot source choices reached only 72.165 MHz\nmedian and is explicitly excluded. The final proposal retains the\noriginal two-bit EX data mux topology while registering its complete\nselection. These partial probes do not establish this final version's\nfitness or full formal correctness.\n\nTarget eight percent fitness gain with low confidence before final\nmeasurement. Expect unchanged 3,989,539 bracketed cycles. Acceptance\nrequires more than 194.15852 iterations/s, about 77.460 MHz at that cycle\ncount. Measure all normal Gowin placement options and actual CoreMark\ncycles, canonical CRC/UART/bounds/markers, real/ALTOPS pipeline and ALU\ntests, unchanged single-channel formal including liveness, and stalled\nreference cosimulation. Never relax an assertion or formal depth.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Replace only MEM/WB alu_result and read_data with one 32-bit selected result. Preserve EX/MEM and every RVFI/control field.'},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Capture the final load-versus-ALU result before the MEM/WB edge and retain it on hold_wb. Export advancing MEM/WB reg_write after alignment traps without valid gating. Keep memory IO, raw RVFI memory data, metadata and hold-valid behavior.'},\n{'file':'rtl/wb_stage.sv','description':'Drive w_data directly from the selected result register, preserving write enable and destination logic.'},\n{'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'Register final two-bit operand selectors, moving next producer qualification and EX-over-WB priority before the edge. Use actual trap-adjusted advancing permissions with existing hold priorities. Refresh every cycle, preserve x0 and retained WB eligibility, and remove post-edge qualification/priority.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Export in.valid && \"'!execute_wait && ctrl_with_trap.reg_write as the normal advancing EX/MEM writer permission. Keep the existing two-bit case-based forwarding muxes, operand overrides, arithmetic, target logic and all acceptance/handshake semantics.'\"'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire the EX and MEM advancing writer permissions into forward_unit. Keep existing WB data consumers, two-bit forwarding selectors and all external ports.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all existing selector observations and architectural assertions.\nAdd or extend mixed ALU/load source-priority cases, simultaneous same-rd\nwriters, x0, trapping loads/jumps, bubbles, flushes and multiply/divide\nholds. Verify producer drain during a held consumer and a retired WB\nload retained through prolonged data stalls, with consumers in EX and\nID. Check every load width/sign, forwarded RVFI operands, unchanged\nlatencies, raw memory data, strict order and exactly-once side effects.\n'''}]}\nclass D(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\nD.add_representer(str,lambda dumper,data:dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',data,style='|' if '\\\\n' in data else None))\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=D,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Final encoding refinement written and schema validated.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
