{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_88","type":"agent_message","text":"The selected MEM/WB result register was the strongest tested option, at roughly +4.0%. I’m keeping that focused change as the final hypothesis and recording that its measured gain falls short of the 4.6% acceptance threshold. The forwarding and recovery variants were weaker."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_89", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r11s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-002-r11s1',\n'title':'Retiming writeback selection into a single MEM/WB result register',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 185.62 iterations/s at 74.054 MHz and 3,989,539\nbracketed CoreMark cycles. After r10s1 shortened competing M-extension\npaths, its worst reported path starts at MEM/WB read_data, crosses WB\nand EX, and ends at the fetch PC. The current MEM/WB boundary registers\nALU and extended load data separately, then selects between them in WB.\nThat late wide mux feeds forwarding, branch/JALR recovery and the\nregister-file write bypass. Structural r8s1 showed that moving work\nacross existing boundaries can help without adding instruction cycles;\nr8s0 also showed that a combinational mux rewrite alone is insufficient.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Move architectural writeback-data selection across the existing MEM/WB\nclock boundary. Replace mem_wb_t's separately registered alu_result and\nread_data with one 32-bit result field. In mem_stage calculate the same\nsign/zero-extended load_data as today, then capture\nin.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result into result on every\nnormal MEM/WB advance edge. wb_stage wires w_data directly to result,\nretaining its existing valid/reg_write write enable and destination.\nThis is one structural register-boundary repartition: there is no new\nstage, execution stall, bypass source or retirement cycle.\n\nAll architectural WB consumers must use this same held result through\nthe existing wb_w_data connections: EX forwarding, same-cycle register\nfile bypass, register writes and RVFI rd_wdata. Keep raw dmem_rdata,\nmasks, address and store data separately for RVFI; never replace raw\nrvfi_mem_rdata with an extended/selected result. Retain every instruction,\noperand, PC and trap field and the existing MEM alignment checks.\nA trapping load still suppresses its write exactly as before. Do not\ngate result capture by valid, reg_write or rd\"'!=0: preserve the original\nWB mux'\"'s value even for bubbles and nonwriters, leaving eligibility to\nexisting control logic.\n\nReset clears result with the rest of MEM/WB. On hold_wb retain result,\nrd and control while clearing valid only. This preserves forwarding\nfrom an already-retired load while a later memory operation stalls:\nMEM/WB.valid may be zero while its data remains an eligible source.\nPreserve current registered forwarding matches, live qualification,\nEX/MEM-over-WB priority and two-bit selector/data mux implementation.\nOlder instructions still drain during blocking multiply/divide and\nsaved RVFI operand semantics remain identical. Keep nret=1, all ports,\ndecoder legality, M-extension and ALTOPS schedules, predictor, fetch\nFIFO, recovery latency and bus acceptance rules unchanged.\n\nThe intended gain is removal of the wide post-register WB mux and\nroughly one 32-bit result register bank from the recurrent feedback\ninterface. The mux instead enters MEM's load-result input cone, where\nmemory extraction could become limiting. Gowin can also expose other\nEX/divider paths, so the transformation does not guarantee acceptance.\nDo not combine it with registered forwarding priority, one-hot operand\nmuxes, delayed branch recovery or validity-only payload changes.\nThose were separate probes and did not outperform this focused design.\n\nThe exact selected-result-only prototype passed lint, both existing\nreal/ALTOPS pipeline builds, stalled reference/selftest cosimulation\nand canonical stalled CoreMark validation. It measured Gowin placement\noptions 0/1/2 at 73.181/77.044/77.044 MHz, median 77.044 MHz, with\n5,874 logic cells, 1,323 registers, 49 LUT-RAMs, four BSRAMs and two DSPs.\nThe original register count was 1,356. The new critical paths bypass\nu_wb and still involve EX/recovery; option 1's leading arrival was\n18.274 ns from MEM/WB.result to a fetch-queue RAM write enable.\nAt unchanged 3,989,539 cycles this projects about 193.12 iterations/s,\napproximately four percent above 185.62. This is BELOW the 4.6 percent\nacceptance gate, not an accepted improvement. The strongest tested\nstructural angle therefore has an evidence-based expected gain of\nfour percent with low confidence, rather than an unsupported larger\nclaim. Full formal was stopped before completion for the proposal\ntime budget; no complete formal pass is claimed.\n\nAcceptance requires more than 194.15852 iterations/s, about 77.460 MHz\nat 3,989,539 cycles. Reproduce actual bracketed CoreMark cycles and the\nnormal three-option median vendor timing after implementation. Run all\nexisting real/ALTOPS unit tests, unchanged single-channel formal with\nliveness and strict order, stalled reference cosimulation, and canonical\nCoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks. Never weaken any assertion,\nformal depth, decoder invariant or evaluation contract. Preserve this\nsingle retiming focus; do not present a best placement or the subthreshold\nprototype projection as an accepted fitness improvement.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':4,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Replace only mem_wb_t.alu_result and mem_wb_t.read_data with a single\n32-bit result field holding final architectural writeback data.\nPreserve ex_mem_t, controls and all RVFI metadata fields.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Capture the existing mem_to_reg selection between extended load_data\nand in.alu_result into MEM/WB.result. Remove the two old result\ncaptures. Clear result on reset and retain it on hold_wb, leaving\nvalid clearing, traps, raw RVFI memory data and memory IO unchanged.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/wb_stage.sv','description':'''Drive w_data directly from MEM/WB.result, removing its late wide mux.\nPreserve w_en = in.ctrl.reg_write && in.valid and w_addr = in.rd.\nUpdate comments to describe selection before the MEM/WB register.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve every existing selector, latency and architectural assertion.\nExtend mixed load/ALU forwarding coverage for all load widths/signs,\nconsumers in EX and ID, same-rd writers, x0 and trapping misaligned\nloads. Check a retired WB load retained while valid clears during a\nprolonged younger dmem stall, with branch/JALR, store and multiply/divide\nconsumers. Assert original RVFI operands, selected result, raw memory\ndata, contiguous order and exactly-once side effects.\n'''}]}\nclass D(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\nD.add_representer(str,lambda dumper,data:dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',data,style='|' if '\\\\n' in data else None))\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=D,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261002-002-r11s1' and d['category']=='structural'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int and -50 <= d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct'] <= 50\nprint('Final hypothesis written and schema validated:',p)\nprint('Prototype fitness:',77.044e6*10/3989539)\nPY\ngit status --short\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
