{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"agent_message","text":"CoreMark profiling shows 135,320 adjacent load-to-branch dependencies, but only 8,949 load-to-simple-ALU dependencies. I’m focusing the final hypothesis on those branches: compare the accepted load response early, register the decision, then recover from that register. This targets the load-use bubble while separating the memory-to-comparator path from frontend recovery."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_56", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r12s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-002-r12s1\ntitle: \\\"Fuse load-to-branch execution through a registered comparison stage\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion achieves 199.08 iterations/s at 79.422 MHz and 3,989,539\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst path now runs from an ID/EX operand\n  through EX branch resolution to the fetch PC. A local run of the\n  unchanged champion with the standard instruction/data stalls reproduced\n  exactly 3,989,539 bracketed cycles. Streaming its retirement trace found\n  135,320 adjacent true load-to-branch dependencies, versus only 8,949\n  load-to-simple-ALU dependencies. Load-to-branch execution is therefore a\n  materially larger throughput opportunity than general late ALU execution.\n\n  Structural r11s1's isolated writeback retiming gained only 4.04 percent;\n  r11s0 removed its companion EX result mux and exposed recovery timing.\n  Earlier branch-only direct MEM-to-EX forwarding saved cycles but lost\n  frequency in r7s0. Selective MEM verification in r10s2 avoided the raw\n  memory feedback path but placed operand repair and comparison on the\n  recovery cycle and lost frequency. Moving every branch to MEM in r8s2\n  failed bounded liveness. The proposed load/branch pair instead performs\n  comparison early and registers its decision before frontend recovery,\n  while independent branches retain their original schedule.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a dedicated load-to-branch comparison lane spanning the existing\n  EX/MEM boundary. Admit a legal conditional branch immediately behind a\n  load it consumes, without the current load-use bubble. While that load\n  is in MEM and the branch is in EX, compare the accepted, correctly\n  extended load response against the branch's other forwarded operand.\n  Capture the outcome and original prediction with the branch in EX/MEM.\n  In the following cycle recover and train from those registered bits.\n  This is one structural change to execution placement: form a load/branch\n  pair with a register between comparison and recovery. Prediction policy,\n  independent-branch resolution and all other instruction latencies stay\n  unchanged. Do not defer the comparison itself to MEM/WB forwarding.\n\n  Restrict admission to positively decoded legal BRANCH encodings whose\n  source matches the preceding valid load's nonzero destination. Exempt\n  these branches from the one-cycle load-use interlock; retain all other\n  interlocks. In EX identify the pair against the actual older EX/MEM load\n  and latch a paired-branch bit only when the branch advances. Backpressure\n  from that load still holds both stages until io_dmemReady is asserted.\n  An instruction-bus stall or empty fetch queue cannot invent a pair.\n  Independent conditional branches, JAL and JALR still resolve in EX.\n\n  Export MEM's existing byte/halfword/word extraction and sign extension\n  as a combinational internal load value, together with actual load-write\n  qualification after alignment/trap checking. Supply that value only to\n  the dedicated pair comparator and its captured RVFI source values. For\n  each matching branch source use the load value if the load really writes\n  that nonzero register. For a trapping misaligned load, use the youngest\n  older architectural value: a matching retained MEM/WB writer, otherwise\n  the ID/EX operand. Exclude the pending load's EX/MEM alu_result, which is\n  an address, from this fallback. Handle either or both branch operands\n  matching the load. Unmatched operands retain ordinary forwarding.\n  MEM/WB forwarding qualification must retain today's behavior when valid\n  clears but its data/control remain available during a data stall.\n\n  Implement a separate six-condition comparator for the paired lane so\n  raw load data never enters the normal EX branch-to-IF path. Compute its\n  EQ, signed ordering and unsigned ordering from the repaired operands.\n  Its decision terminates at EX/MEM outcome/fault/prediction metadata,\n  not at IF PC selection, queue reset, ID squash or predictor RAM writes.\n  Register the full direct target and branch PC independently of that\n  decision, reusing existing EX/MEM fields. MEM recovery chooses the\n  registered target or registered PC plus four from registered outcome\n  bits. Avoid using an outcome-selected wide PC register as the timed\n  destination of the raw-load comparator. RVFI-only resolved pc_next may\n  be captured conventionally; derive timed recovery from the separate\n  registered candidates and decision. Keep full 32-bit address arithmetic.\n\n  Check target alignment exactly as today: a taken misaligned target traps,\n  suppresses the architectural transfer and continues at PC+4; a not-taken\n  branch with an unaligned encoded target remains legal. Carry corrected\n  original source values, resolved next PC, trap controls and instruction\n  identity through normal retirement. Suppress EX redirect and training\n  for paired branches. In MEM an accepted paired branch compares its saved\n  prediction with its saved legal aligned actual-taken bit, then emits\n  recovery and eligible conditional training once. Unrelated dmem-not-ready\n  cannot hold this branch because its MEM packet has no memory request.\n  Normal ALU, multiply, divide, memory-address and store-data paths do not\n  consume the new raw-load forwarding signal.\n\n  An older paired-branch MEM correction wins over all younger EX activity.\n  It advances the branch to WB, discards the fetch queue, flushes ID/EX and\n  installs an inert EX/MEM bubble instead of the younger EX instruction.\n  Gate younger multiply/divide launch, redirect, training and acceptance\n  with this older kill. Kill must win over a wrong-path execute_wait;\n  clearing valid alone is insufficient because controls also drive memory\n  enables and forwarding. Older work still retires once, and no wrong-path\n  memory operation reaches EX/MEM. Preserve ordinary dmem hold priorities\n  when no older correction exists. Correctly predicted paired branches\n  allow younger execution to continue normally.\n\n  Feed the existing single registered predictor-training interface with\n  accepted MEM-pair learning ahead of accepted EX-branch learning. If both\n  eligible events coincide on a correct path, deliberately discard only\n  the younger advisory EX learning event, as in the earlier selective\n  verification design; do not stall execution or add a second table port.\n  Never train a squashed instruction. Preserve the current counter table,\n  indexing, reset retention, saturation and pre-edge prediction semantics.\n  Account for this explicit collision policy when comparing cycle counts.\n\n  The schedule is essential: with zero wait states, the old load-use bubble\n  places the dependent branch in EX on the same cycle that the new paired\n  branch reaches MEM. Thus its registered recovery is available on the\n  original recovery cycle. A correct prediction saves a bubble; a miss\n  does not acquire the additional recovery cycle of the failed all-MEM\n  design. On a stalled load, sample the comparison only at its accepted\n  response edge. This timing argument does not replace the unchanged\n  liveness proof; keep every formal bound and assumption intact.\n\n  Estimate a seven percent fitness improvement with low confidence. The\n  measured 135,320 adjacent dependencies represent at most 3.39 percent\n  of current cycles, not a guaranteed saving: bus stalls and predictions\n  change the realized schedule. At three percent fewer cycles, acceptance\n  above 208.23768 iterations/s requires about 80.585 MHz median Fmax, only\n  1.46 percent above the champion. Registering the early comparison is\n  intended to avoid the frequency losses of both earlier unregistered\n  memory forwarding and late WB-repair/comparison. Added comparator area,\n  load-data fanout, recovery arbitration, and the unchanged ordinary EX\n  branch path may still prevent acceptance. No implementation, timing or\n  correctness result is claimed for this final paired design.\n\n  Preliminary alternatives were rejected rather than included: a parallel\n  forwarding-source comparator bank passed all 36 pipeline tests and a\n  combinational equivalence proof but measured only 72.148 MHz in Gowin\n  placement option 1. Registered queue-recovery bookkeeping also failed\n  to establish a gain. The final hypothesis is the load/branch execution\n  pairing above, not either measured below-threshold alternative.\n\n  Preserve nret=1, every top-level IO, decoder default-illegal behavior,\n  RV32M/ALTOPS behavior, memory handshakes and all RVFI invariants. Validate\n  the pair's temporal behavior and exact architectural traces with unit\n  tests, unchanged formal including liveness, and stalled cosimulation.\n  Run canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks and every scored\n  Gowin placement option. Inspect both raw-load-to-decision-register and\n  ordinary/deferred recovery paths; evaluate the product of measured\n  iterations/cycle and median Fmax, not isolated clock or bubble savings.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Recognize legal conditional branches consuming the preceding valid\n      load and admit only those through the load-use interlock. Preserve\n      all other dependency and bus holds. Give an older paired-branch MEM\n      correction priority over younger execute_wait and flush all younger\n      state with inert controls, without freezing the resolving branch.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add paired-branch, saved prediction, registered actual-taken and any\n      needed recovery/fault metadata to ex_mem_t. Reuse its PC, full direct\n      target and operand fields. Keep target candidate capture independent\n      of the paired comparator and preserve the external RVFI shape.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Detect an actual load/branch pair at EX acceptance. Build repaired\n      paired operands from MEM's accepted load value or a correctly\n      qualified older fallback and compare them in a dedicated lane.\n      Register the outcome and RVFI operands in EX/MEM; suppress immediate\n      EX recovery/training for this case. Keep ordinary branch and jump\n      execution unchanged. Accept an older MEM kill with priority over\n      holds, clearing outgoing controls and suppressing both M-extension\n      requests, redirects, training and younger architectural effects.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose existing extended load data and actual load-write qualification\n      internally, preserving bus and trap semantics. For a paired branch,\n      generate accepted recovery and learning solely from registered\n      metadata; choose its registered target or PC+4 without recomparing\n      repaired WB operands. Retire its captured architectural result and\n      source values once through the unchanged MEM/WB boundary. Preserve\n      WB data/control retention and every ordinary memory operation.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire internal load value/write qualification and pending-load identity\n      into the pair lane, plus retained WB fallback metadata. Merge recovery\n      by age, giving MEM-pair correction priority over younger EX actions.\n      Arbitrate the existing training input with MEM learning ahead of EX\n      learning, deliberately dropping only a colliding younger advisory\n      update. Leave external ports and the existing fetch predictor intact.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve existing architectural assertions and extend the observer\n      for selective registered pair recovery and its explicit learning\n      collision policy. Test every branch condition and load width/sign,\n      either/both matching sources, x0, competing older writers, trapping\n      misaligned loads with fallback, and target faults. Check no load-use\n      bubble on warm correct predictions and the original load/branch\n      recovery edge on misses; independent branches must retain EX timing.\n      Exercise long load stalls, instruction stalls, full-queue recovery,\n      reset, and wrong-path store/load/JALR/MUL/DIV after a pair. Verify no\n      younger launch or side effect, exact RVFI operands/PCs/traps/order,\n      captured decision stability, once-only retirement/training, and the\n      unchanged bounded progress checks for load/branch/divide sequences.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r12s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261002-002-r12s1' and d['category']=='structural'\nassert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Schema valid:',p)\nprint('Fmax threshold with 3% cycle reduction:',199.08*1.046*3989539*.97/1e7)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
