{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_23", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ('hyp-20261002-001-r8s0','hyp-20261002-002-r8s1'):\n  print(d['id']);print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis']);print(d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-001-r8s0\nReplace the internal two-bit forwarding encoding with three mutually\nexclusive source enables, then directly select ALU operands from the\nfour possible sources. This is one EX operand-selection micro-optimization.\nPreserve the pipeline, divider, multiplier arithmetic, fetch queue,\npredictor, instruction latency and every architectural interface.\n\nPrecompute each writer's eligibility at its existing pipeline boundary.\nAdd forward_eligible to ex_mem_t and mem_wb_t. In EX capture\nctrl_with_trap.reg_write && (in.rd!=0) with EX/MEM; in MEM capture\nctrl_with_trap.reg_write && (in.rd!=0) with MEM/WB, using MEM's final\nmisalignment-adjusted controls. Reset/bubble clears clear the bit;\na dmem hold preserves it, including when MEM/WB.valid is cleared.\nDo not include valid in either eligibility expression. These bits\nregister exactly the existing writer qualification, moving its\nnonzero check off the late forwarding-control path.\n\nFor each source register compute ex_match and wb_match from these\nregistered eligibility bits and the existing destination/source tag\nequality. Remove the now-redundant combinational rd!=0 tests from\nforward_unit; its write-enable inputs now mean prequalified eligibility.\nDefine use_ex=ex_match, use_wb=!ex_match && wb_match, and\nuse_id=!ex_match && !wb_match. Export these as a three-bit vector with\nbit 0 selecting ID/EX, bit 1 EX/MEM, and bit 2 MEM/WB. Do not qualify\nMEM/WB forwarding with its valid bit: its retained data and controls\nintentionally remain available while data-memory backpressure clears\nretirement validity. Preserve EX/MEM priority and the current x0 rules.\n\nIn EX form ordinary rs1 and rs2 with a masked OR of the three source\nvalues. These remain the values used by branch comparisons, effective\naddress generation, store data, divider source capture and RVFI.\nCrucially, do not cascade these rs muxes into the ALU operand muxes.\nBuild alu_a directly as four masked terms: in.pc when is_auipc,\notherwise each of in.rs1_val, fwd_ex_mem and fwd_mem_wb gated by its\none\n…[7790 of 10862 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:294ceab36a541aba]…\nhmetic and the shortened\ndivider recurrence, approximately 21.06 ns arrival. The combined design\ncompleted canonical stalled CoreMark in exactly 3,977,318 bracketed\ncycles, passing all CRC/UART/bounds/completion/marker validation. This\nprojects 161.3147 iterations/s, 12.54 percent above 143.34. All existing\nALU tests plus an added radix-8 boundary test passed in real and ALTOPS\nbuilds (31 tests each), retaining exact latency checks; both pipeline\ntest variants passed all 27 tests. Reference cosimulation passed\nselftest plus three random programs with and without stalls. All 53\nunchanged ALTOPS formal checks passed, including reg_ch0, pc_fwd,\nliveness and unique; lint also passed. ALTOPS does not prove the real\nradix-8 arithmetic, which the real-arithmetic vectors exercise.\nThese are prototype results, not\norchestrator acceptance. Reproduce unchanged correctness and median\nvendor timing gates after implementation; do not substitute a faster\nplacement option for the median or omit CoreMark cycle measurement.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
