'  input  logic [2:0]         fwd_rs1_sel,','  input logic [3:0] fwd_alu_a, fwd_alu_b,\\\\n  input  logic [2:0]         fwd_rs1_sel,');s=s.replace('in.ctrl.is_auipc}} & in.pc','fwd_alu_a[3]}} & in.pc').replace('in.ctrl.alu_src}} & in.imm','fwd_alu_b[3]}} & in.imm');\nfor k in range(3):s=s.replace(f'\"'!in.ctrl.is_auipc && fwd_rs1_sel[{k}]'\"',f'fwd_alu_a[{k}]').replace(f'\"'!in.ctrl.alu_src && fwd_rs2_sel[{k}]'\"',f'fwd_alu_b[{k}]')\np.write_text(s)\np=dst/'core.sv';s=p.read_text().replace('  logic next_ex_w_en, next_wb_w_en;','  logic next_ex_w_en, next_wb_w_en;\\\\n  logic decoded_auipc, decoded_alu_src;\\\\n  logic [3:0] fwd_alu_a, fwd_alu_b;');s=s.replace('    .out      (id_ex_w)','    .decoded_auipc(decoded_auipc), .decoded_alu_src(decoded_alu_src),\\\\n    .out      (id_ex_w)');s=s.replace('    .fwd_rs1_sel     (fwd_rs1_sel),','    .fwd_alu_a(fwd_alu_a), .fwd_alu_b(fwd_alu_b),\\\\n    .fwd_rs1_sel     (fwd_rs1_sel),');s=s.replace('    .fwd_rs1     (fwd_rs1_sel),','    .next_is_auipc(stall_id ? id_ex_w.ctrl.is_auipc : decoded_auipc),\\\\n    .next_alu_src(stall_id ? id_ex_w.ctrl.alu_src : decoded_alu_src),\\\\n    .fwd_alu_a(fwd_alu_a), .fwd_alu_b(fwd_alu_b),\\\\n    .fwd_rs1     (fwd_rs1_sel),');p.write_text(s)\nPY\npython - <<'PY' > /srv/hwebench/tmp/9e6b10b9/fed74f44/r13s1-fullroute-timing.log 2>&1\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/9e6b10b9/fed74f44')\nr=build_all(Path('.').resolve(),p/'r13s1-fullroute-rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',p/'r13s1-fullroute-timing')\nprint(json.dumps({'summary':summarize(r),'results':r},indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_52", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r13s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261002-002-r13s1\ntitle: \\\"Register complete operand routes before the EX stage\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 199.08 iterations/s at 79.422 MHz and 3,989,539\n  measured CoreMark cycles. Structural r8s1 registered forwarding address\n  matches but left producer qualification and priority after the edge.\n  The r8s0 flat operand mux retained same-cycle address comparison and did\n  not improve fitness; r12s2 still exposed forwarding-select-to-ALU-result\n  timing. The existing boundary therefore registers only part of the\n  routing decision. Moving its complete decision before the EX edge can\n  shorten both arithmetic and branch operand paths without extra latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retiming the complete operand-routing control into registers alongside\n  ID/EX will replace the current registered-address-match plus live\n  qualification, priority encoder, source mux and PC/immediate override.\n  Keep the five-stage pipeline, data registers, load-use interlock,\n  predictor, fetch queue and blocking M-extension schedules unchanged.\n  This is one structural relocation of the operand-routing boundary.\n\n  Compute the exact identities and write controls of producers that will\n  occupy EX/MEM and MEM/WB AFTER the next edge. Reuse next_rs1/next_rs2,\n  next_ex_rd and next_wb_rd from the current forward_unit. The next EX/MEM\n  write control is its retained reg_write during a dmem hold, zero when\n  EX emits a bubble or waits for M completion, otherwise the actual\n  ctrl_with_trap.reg_write about to be captured. The next MEM/WB write\n  control is its retained reg_write during hold_mem_wb, otherwise MEM's\n  post-alignment ctrl_with_trap.reg_write. Do not qualify retained WB\n  forwarding with retirement valid; its data is still a legitimate source.\n  Expose these next-state controls through internal EX and MEM ports.\n  Do not guess eligibility from opcode or omit fault suppression.\n\n  Before the edge, compare each next consumer source with these producer\n  identities, exclude x0, apply actual next write eligibility, and resolve\n  youngest EX/MEM priority over MEM/WB. Register the FINAL mutually\n  exclusive route for each source: bit 0 selects ID/EX's saved operand,\n  bit 1 EX/MEM.alu_result and bit 2 MEM/WB.wb_data. Refresh routes on every\n  nonreset edge, even while ID/EX holds, because older producers can move\n  or drain. Reset selects the saved operand. Remove the old registered\n  match bits and all functional post-edge write qualification, priority\n  encoding and selector decoding. Forwarded values themselves remain\n  in their current registers; no live MEM load data is fed into EX.\n\n  Also register complete four-way ALU routes at this same boundary.\n  Expose the ID decoder's actual is_auipc and alu_src signals internally.\n  When ID/EX holds, use its retained controls; otherwise use the decoded\n  incoming controls, with zero controls for an actual flushed/invalid\n  packet. ALU A selects PC if next_is_auipc, else the registered next rs1\n  route. ALU B selects immediate if next_alu_src, else the registered next\n  rs2 route. These are four-bit one-hot registers with bits 0/1/2 denoting\n  saved operand/EX-MEM/MEM-WB and bit 3 denoting PC or immediate. Resolve\n  all override gating BEFORE the register, not by qualifying one-hot\n  source bits with live ID/EX controls afterward. Preserve the existing\n  hold-before-flush priority and reset every route to saved operand.\n\n  In EX use shallow masked-OR selectors driven directly by those route\n  registers. Raw rs1/rs2 use the three-way routes for branch comparison,\n  addresses, store data and RVFI; ALU A/B use the four-way routes directly,\n  avoiding a raw-source mux followed by a PC/immediate mux. Use balanced\n  pairwise OR grouping. Keep ordinary ALU logic and the dedicated full\n  rs1-plus-immediate address calculation unchanged. In particular AUIPC\n  must not replace raw RVFI rs1 with PC, and immediate operations must not\n  replace raw store/branch/RVFI rs2 with the immediate. Preserve all real\n  and ALTOPS arithmetic, captured M operands, completion backpressure,\n  recovery/training timing, valid/control bubble clearing and nret=1 IO.\n\n  This differs from registering mere eligibility bits or address matches:\n  source matching, producer suppression, youngest-writer arbitration and\n  ALU operand overrides all terminate at the routing registers. It also\n  avoids replicating arithmetic datapaths or adding pipeline stages.\n  Expect eight percent higher fitness at identical CoreMark cycles, with\n  low confidence until vendor timing confirms the complete design.\n  Acceptance requires more than 208.23768 iterations/s, about 83.08 MHz\n  at 3,989,539 cycles. Branch comparison, multiplier/divider paths and the\n  new next-route setup paths can limit the gain; inspect all scored\n  placement options rather than extrapolating from mux depth alone.\n\n  Check every next-route transition against the original forwarding\n  priority, including simultaneous writers, x0, invalid/bubbled producers,\n  faulting JAL/JALR, misaligned loads, load-use bubbles, dmem holds and\n  M waits while older instructions drain. Check complete raw and ALU\n  routes are one-hot and aligned with the same consumer after capture,\n  hold, flush and reset. Preserve all architectural pipeline assertions;\n  update only implementation-specific selector-encoding observations to\n  check the corresponding one-hot route and exact selected value. Run\n  unchanged formal checks including reg, unique, pc_fwd and liveness,\n  real/ALTOPS unit tests, stalled reference cosimulation and canonical\n  CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks. Require unchanged measured cycles\n  and measure median Gowin Fmax before claiming a fitness improvement.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace registered matches and live qualification/priority with\n      fully registered three-bit raw-source routes and four-bit ALU routes.\n      Use exact next producer write controls, next source/destination\n      identities and next consumer decode controls. Refresh on holds;\n      preserve retained WB forwarding, youngest priority and x0 behavior.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose actual combinational is_auipc and alu_src decode controls for\n      pre-edge route formation. Preserve all data-register capture, hold,\n      flush/reset and default-illegal decoder behavior.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export exact next EX/MEM reg_write eligibility after stall, bubble,\n      completion and trap handling. Replace raw and ALU operand mux chains\n      with balanced masked-OR selectors driven by registered routes.\n      Preserve arithmetic, address, branch and M-extension behavior.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export exact next MEM/WB reg_write after alignment/trap suppression,\n      retaining current eligibility while hold_mem_wb clears only valid.\n      Preserve load extraction, WB result capture and every bus effect.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire next producer eligibility and incoming/held decode controls to\n      the routing registers; connect raw and final ALU one-hot routes to\n      EX. Remove obsolete forwarding qualification ports. Preserve hazard\n      timing and external memory/RVFI interfaces with nret=1.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain architectural and timing checks while adapting internal\n      forwarding-encoding assertions to one-hot routes and exact values.\n      Add route-transition tests covering competing/faulting writers,\n      every hold/flush/reset combination, load-use and M waits, x0,\n      PC/immediate overrides and original raw RVFI/store operands.\n''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated hypothesis is schema-valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
