{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport shutil\np=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/9e6b10b9/d731bb2d/rebase_probe')\nf=p/'formal'; f.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nfor name in ['run_all.sh','wrapper_si.sv','checks_si.cfg']:\n shutil.copy2(Path('formal')/name,f/name)\nup=Path('formal/riscv-formal').resolve()\nfor name in ['checks','insns']:\n shutil.copytree(up/name,f/'riscv-formal'/name,dirs_exist_ok=True)\nPY\nRTL_DIR=rtl CORE_NAME=bench WRAPPER=/srv/hwebench/tmp/9e6b10b9/d731bb2d/rebase_probe/formal/wrapper_si.sv CHECKS_CFG=/srv/hwebench/tmp/9e6b10b9/d731bb2d/rebase_probe/formal/checks_si.cfg JOBS=8 bash /srv/hwebench/tmp/9e6b10b9/d731bb2d/rebase_probe/formal/run_all.sh > /srv/hwebench/tmp/9e6b10b9/d731bb2d/rebase_probe/formal.log 2>&1\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r14s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Register recovery selection and rebase fetch while squashing the younger EX instruction'\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'}\nd['hypothesis']='''Retiming branch recovery across the EX acceptance edge can remove the late\nforwarded-comparison-to-front-end control path without adding a recovery\ncycle. Register a recovery event and its correction address, then use that\nregistered event to select the logical fetch PC and bypass stale queued\ninstructions immediately after the same edge. Move the younger instruction's\nsquash point from ID capture to EX acceptance. This is one coordinated\npipeline topology change, preserving the current instruction schedule,\nbranch comparator, predictor policy, arithmetic and retirement width.\n\nLet E be an edge where a control transfer is accepted into EX/MEM. Compute\nthe existing legal/aligned prediction-error condition and capture it in\nrecovery_valid_q at E. Do not send the live condition to IF, ID, hazards,\nqueue write enables or pipeline flush controls. The resolving instruction\nadvances normally with its original actual pc_next, trap/link results and\nRVFI operands. Gate acceptance on reset, valid, older memory backpressure,\nexecute_wait and \"'!recovery_valid_q, so killed younger controls cannot create\nanother recovery or training event.\n\nCapture recovery_target_q every clock independently of recovery validity.\nIts input is (forwarded_rs1+immediate)&~1 for JALR; for direct control it is\npredicted_taken ? PC+4 : PC+immediate. This alternative to the original\nprediction is the correct recovery target whenever the existing mismatch\ncondition fires. Avoid a late actual_taken mux or recovery-dependent enable\non this payload register. Architectural pc_next still records the actual\nsuccessor and original misalignment/trap behavior. Reset clears recovery\nvalidity; invalid target payload need not be reset.\n\nIF keeps normal_pc_q, but its logical PC is recovery_valid_q ?\nrecovery_target_q : normal_pc_q. Use that same logical PC for the external\nfetch address, prediction indexing, fetch-packet PC and next-PC arithmetic.\nThus immediately after E the corrected address is already visible, just as\nin the champion. During the registered recovery cycle, present the live\ncorrected fetch packet regardless of stale FIFO occupancy. Keep the actual\nFIFO read address at rd_ptr: do not first mux it to zero using recovery,\nsince that reconstructs a recovery-to-LUT-RAM-to-hazard path before the head\nbypass. Stale queue contents are discarded logically by the output bypass\nand physically when count and pointers clear at E+1.\n\nAt E the younger ID instruction may enter ID/EX; during E..E+1 the registered\nevent kills every one of its EX effects. Suppress its execute_wait, MUL/DIV\nrequests, completion consumption, training and recovery creation. At E+1\nwrite an EX/MEM bubble with all controls and metadata zero; merely clearing\nvalid is unsafe because memory and forwarding inspect controls separately.\nThe older resolving instruction in MEM/WB still completes exactly once.\nRetain the existing unused alu_result capture during bubbles for timing.\n\nUnlike the initial delayed-recovery experiment, do NOT flush ID/EX when\nthe registered event is high: ID is now looking at the corrected target.\nIt captures that target at E+1 whenever imemReady is high. Suppress load-use\nhazards from the killed younger EX instruction, and suppress its arithmetic\nwait before generating hazard control. The resolving older instruction in\nEX/MEM is a branch or jump, never a load/store, so there is no genuine older\nmemory hold in this reachable cycle. Prove this property and retain normal\nolder-memory priority everywhere else. A branch waiting behind memory may\ncreate neither recovery nor training until that memory operation advances.\n\nUse the following explicit IF state transition to avoid feedback through\nwrong-path occupancy and hazard logic. In a recovery cycle fetch_accept is\nimemReady, dequeue and enqueue are false, and the queue count/read/write\npointers clear at its ending edge. The corrected word is consumed directly\nwhen ready; it needs no FIFO entry because recovery suppresses all possible\nyounger execution holds. Set normal_pc_q to imemReady ? next_pc :\nrecovery_target_q on that edge. This preserves the corrected address if the\nbus is stalled when the one-cycle selector returns to normal. Outside\nrecovery, keep the current four-entry fall-through queue transitions and\nnormal_pc_q update on fetch_accept. Equivalent equations are:\n  consume = out.valid && !stall;\n  dequeue = consume && count!=0 && !recovery_valid_q;\n  fetch_accept = imemReady && (recovery_valid_q || count<4 || dequeue);\n  enqueue = fetch_accept && !recovery_valid_q && (count!=0 || !consume).\nReset takes priority and selects RESET_PC with an empty queue. Keep all\naddresses 32 bits and preserve full-queue simultaneous pop/push behavior.\n\nKeep the accepted-branch training event at its original edge and retain the\nexisting one-cycle training register, counter initialization, saturation and\nruntime-reset history. Only real accepted branches train; the younger killed\ninstruction cannot train, request arithmetic, write memory/registers or\nretire. Preserve the balanced branch comparator, forwarding and retained-WB\nrules, dedicated effective address, multiplier/divider schedules and ALTOPS\nsemantics. Leave decoder validation, trap discipline, nret=1 and all external\nIO/RVFI interfaces unchanged. Do not add another recovery cycle, arithmetic\nredesign, predictor policy, cache, or load-to-branch bypass.\n\nPreliminary disposable experiments identify why the complete topology is\nneeded. Simply registering recovery and applying the PC at the following\nedge measured median 85.259 MHz and 4,055,160 CoreMark cycles versus the\nchampion'\"'s 84.388 MHz and 3,989,539 cycles; it also failed unchanged liveness.\nThat version is rejected, not proposed. A version that selected the corrected\nPC immediately and bypassed stale occupancy restored exactly 3,989,539 cycles\nand passed all eight reference-cosimulation runs. It reached only 83.651 MHz:\nits worst option-1 path ran from the recovery register through a needless\nFIFO read-pointer mux, LUT RAM, hazard logic and fetch acceptance back into\nnormal_pc_q. The final proposal specifically removes that read-pointer mux\nand bypasses acceptance/PC holding directly during recovery using the proved\nno-older-memory/no-younger-wait property. It is not a resubmission of either\nmeasured subthreshold version.\n\nThe eight percent impact estimate is a hypothesis, with low confidence until\nfinal timing and correctness gates reproduce it. At unchanged 3,989,539\nbracketed cycles acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax above about 88.27 MHz\nand fitness above 221.24992 iterations/s. General WB-to-EX ALU and divider\nfeedback remain competing paths, so register insertion alone is insufficient\nevidence of improvement. Run all scored placements and inspect their worst\npaths. Require unchanged bracketed CoreMark cycles, canonical CRCs, UART\nvalidation literal, bounds and timing markers.\n\nExtend directed pipeline checks for the changed internal squash boundary\nwhile retaining all architectural assertions. Cover both miss directions,\ncorrect predictions, JAL/JALR/link dependencies, target faults, both FIFO\nextremes, bus stalls during and after recovery, forwarded branch operands,\nreset during recovery, and older memory draining exactly once. Put stores,\nloads, illegal words, MUL/DIV and another control transfer immediately after\nthe resolving instruction and prove they have no wrong-path effects. Check\nthat the corrected target can enter ID/EX on E+1, stalled corrected fetches\nhold their PC, counter updates occur once, and RVFI orders/PCs/operands/traps\nremain exact. Run the unchanged full formal suite, especially liveness,\npc_fwd, reg and unique; never relax proof depth or verification contracts.\n'''\nd['changes']=[\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Register accepted recovery validity and an independently sampled alternative correction target. Export only registered recovery. Use it to kill all effects and waits of the younger ID/EX instruction before EX/MEM, without changing the older branch architectural result or original training edge. Preserve original arithmetic and complete bubble controls.'},\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Select the logical fetch PC from normal and registered correction state. Bypass stale queue payload directly during recovery without changing the physical read pointer. Consume the ready corrected word directly, clear old queue state at the ending edge, and explicitly capture predicted successor or held corrected address into normal_pc_q. Preserve ordinary FIFO and predictor behavior.'},\n {'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Use registered recovery to suppress load-use hazards from the killed EX instruction. Remove recovery from ID flush because the presented head is already corrected. Preserve real memory holds, ordinary load-use bubbles and waits; prove that ready corrected fetches have no recovery-cycle execution hold.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Connect the registered recovery selection to IF and hazard control with no live branch-verification path to front-end state. Preserve nret=1 IO, retirement and bus wiring. Document the EX kill versus corrected ID capture boundary.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Adapt internal recovery/FIFO models to registered logical rebasing and the moved squash boundary while retaining all architectural checks. Add complete wrong-path effect suppression, corrected-head capture on the original edge, stalled target retention, reset, target faults, queue extremes and forwarding cases. Check exact-once training/retirement, complete RVFI and unchanged proof bounds.'}\n]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\nprint('Rewrote complete hypothesis for zero-extra-cycle recovery topology')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
