{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'make lint TARGET=bench'","aggregated_output":"%Warning-WIDTHEXPAND: cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv:87:48: Operator LT expects 32 bits on the LHS, but LHS's VARREF 'count_q' generates 3 bits.\n                                                       : ... note: In instance 'core.u_if'\n   87 |   assign fetch_accept = imem_ready && (count_q < DEPTH || dequeue);\n      |                                                ^\n                      ... For warning description see https://verilator.org/warn/WIDTHEXPAND?v=5.047\n                      ... Use \"/* verilator lint_off WIDTHEXPAND */\" and lint_on around source to disable this message.\n%Error: Exiting due to 1 warning(s)\nmake: *** [Makefile:98: lint] Error 1\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv');p.write_text(p.read_text().replace('count_q < DEPTH','count_q < 3\\\\'d4'))\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r7s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261002-002-r7s1\ntitle: \\\"Decouple fetch with a four-entry fall-through instruction queue\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 136.42 iterations/s at 57.319 MHz with 4,201,709\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its fetch PC currently advances only when\n  decode accepts the instruction. Load-use bubbles, blocking divides\n  and data-memory stalls therefore waste otherwise-ready fetch cycles,\n  despite independent instruction backpressure on roughly 22 percent\n  of cycles. Prior structural arithmetic changes face a divider timing\n  ceiling: a disposable MEM-stage multiplier prototype removed the EX\n  multiply cone but measured only 56.463 MHz median. A small elastic\n  queue instead targets wasted fetch opportunities and iterations/cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert a four-entry fall-through instruction queue inside if_stage.\n  Separate the speculative fetch PC from the PC of the oldest instruction\n  presented to decode. Keep the existing direct BRANCH/JAL predictor and\n  train it from accepted EX branches exactly as before. Fetch computes\n  its next predicted PC and advances whenever a ready bus word can be\n  consumed directly or enqueued, independently of downstream load-use,\n  divide and data-memory holds. Each queue entry stores the complete\n  instruction, its own PC and the prediction actually used at fetch.\n  Decode and RVFI must use the head entry's PC, never the advanced fetch PC.\n\n  Use a four-entry circular buffer, two-bit read/write pointers and a\n  three-bit occupancy count. Empty queue output bypasses the live bus\n  word and imemReady, preserving the current zero-extra-cycle path.\n  Nonempty output comes from the oldest registered entry and remains\n  valid even while io_imemReady is low. Define consume as head-valid\n  and decode-can-accept. With no redirect/reset, dequeue is consume\n  and count\"'!=0; fetch_accept is io_imemReady and (count<4 or dequeue);\n  enqueue is fetch_accept and (count!=0 or !consume). Empty bypass\n  consumption advances fetch PC without touching occupancy or pointers.\n  Otherwise capture on enqueue, advance the write pointer, and advance\n  the read pointer on dequeue. Update count by enqueue minus dequeue.\n  At full occupancy permit simultaneous dequeue/enqueue; decode must\n  see the old head while the edge writes the replacement. No packet\n  may be dropped, duplicated or overwritten while blocked.\n\n  Reinterpret the existing fetch stall as a block on head consumption,\n  not a blanket freeze of the fetch producer. In core.sv feed queued\n  head validity, rather than raw io_imemReady, to the hazard unit'\"'s\n  instruction-availability input. Preserve the existing load-use source\n  comparisons on the presented head instruction, ID/EX hold/bubble\n  priorities and all dmem/divider control. Thus a ready queued head can\n  advance while the external instruction bus stalls, and a blocked head\n  can accumulate additional predicted-path words while space remains.\n  When the queue is full and cannot dequeue, hold io_imemAddr stable.\n\n  Accepted EX recovery overrides all queue and bus activity: set fetch\n  PC to redirect_target and invalidate the entire queue on that edge,\n  with no enqueue/dequeue accepted. The existing ID/EX flush kills the\n  presented younger instruction. This also works when imemReady is zero\n  or the queue is full. Reset clears occupancy and pointers, resets PC\n  and predictor as before, and need not reset invalid queue payloads.\n  Wrong-path prefetches have no architectural effects and must never\n  retire, train, launch a divide, or access data memory. Preserve the\n  exact nret=1 ports, precise single retirement and +1 order sequence,\n  decoder legality, RV32M arithmetic/ALTOPS, memory bounds and handshakes.\n  This proposal changes front-end elasticity only: no cache, predictor\n  policy change, arithmetic redesign, or extra pipeline stage.\n\n  Expect a 7 percent net fitness gain with medium confidence, pending\n  measurements. Fetch-run-ahead may hide independent bus stalls during\n  existing execution bubbles, while empty fall-through avoids adding a\n  fixed recovery or instruction latency. A new head-selection mux and\n  wider predicted-path buffering can affect timing and wrong-path traffic;\n  the queue may also remain empty when fetch bandwidth is the bottleneck.\n  Measure actual canonical CoreMark cycles and all three Gowin options.\n  Require fitness above 142.69532 iterations/s, with the unchanged five\n  CRCs, UART success literal, in-bounds accesses and MMIO timing markers.\n  Run the existing unit, formal and stalled reference-cosimulation gates\n  without weakening bounds, assumptions or architectural assertions.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the four-entry fall-through circular instruction queue and\n      separate producer fetch-PC advancement from consumer acceptance.\n      Buffer PC, instruction and predicted_taken atomically; support\n      simultaneous enqueue/dequeue, full and empty transitions, and\n      reset/recovery invalidation. Keep predictor policy unchanged.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire queue head availability to the existing instruction hazard\n      input while retaining raw io_imemReady for the fetch producer.\n      Preserve top-level IO, ID/EX acceptance, backpressure and RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Document that the existing instruction-ready input now denotes\n      decode-head availability and stall_if blocks head consumption.\n      Retain load-use, divider, recovery and memory priority equations.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add queue fill/drain, simultaneous full pop/push, empty bypass,\n      long imem/dmem stalls, load-use and divide holds, wraparound,\n      redirects with a full queue or stalled bus, reset and dependent\n      branch/JALR cases. Check no lost/duplicate instructions, precise\n      RVFI PC/source data, retirement ordering and exactly-once stores.\n      Preserve architectural expectations; update only observations that\n      equated speculative io_imemAddr with the decode-head PC. Prove\n      that queued instructions drain during imem stalls and fetch can\n      run ahead during downstream holds, without changing the harness.\n''')\nprint('Replaced hypothesis with the fetch-queue proposal.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv');p.write_text(p.read_text().replace('count_q < DEPTH','count_q < 3\\\\'d4'))\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r7s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261002-002-r7s1\ntitle: \\\"Decouple fetch with a four-entry fall-through instruction queue\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 136.42 iterations/s at 57.319 MHz with 4,201,709\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its fetch PC currently advances only when\n  decode accepts the instruction. Load-use bubbles, blocking divides\n  and data-memory stalls therefore waste otherwise-ready fetch cycles,\n  despite independent instruction backpressure on roughly 22 percent\n  of cycles. Prior structural arithmetic changes face a divider timing\n  ceiling: a disposable MEM-stage multiplier prototype removed the EX\n  multiply cone but measured only 56.463 MHz median. A small elastic\n  queue instead targets wasted fetch opportunities and iterations/cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert a four-entry fall-through instruction queue inside if_stage.\n  Separate the speculative fetch PC from the PC of the oldest instruction\n  presented to decode. Keep the existing direct BRANCH/JAL predictor and\n  train it from accepted EX branches exactly as before. Fetch computes\n  its next predicted PC and advances whenever a ready bus word can be\n  consumed directly or enqueued, independently of downstream load-use,\n  divide and data-memory holds. Each queue entry stores the complete\n  instruction, its own PC and the prediction actually used at fetch.\n  Decode and RVFI must use the head entry's PC, never the advanced fetch PC.\n\n  Use a four-entry circular buffer, two-bit read/write pointers and a\n  three-bit occupancy count. Empty queue output bypasses the live bus\n  word and imemReady, preserving the current zero-extra-cycle path.\n  Nonempty output comes from the oldest registered entry and remains\n  valid even while io_imemReady is low. Define consume as head-valid\n  and decode-can-accept. With no redirect/reset, dequeue is consume\n  and count\"'!=0; fetch_accept is io_imemReady and (count<4 or dequeue);\n  enqueue is fetch_accept and (count!=0 or !consume). Empty bypass\n  consumption advances fetch PC without touching occupancy or pointers.\n  Otherwise capture on enqueue, advance the write pointer, and advance\n  the read pointer on dequeue. Update count by enqueue minus dequeue.\n  At full occupancy permit simultaneous dequeue/enqueue; decode must\n  see the old head while the edge writes the replacement. No packet\n  may be dropped, duplicated or overwritten while blocked.\n\n  Reinterpret the existing fetch stall as a block on head consumption,\n  not a blanket freeze of the fetch producer. In core.sv feed queued\n  head validity, rather than raw io_imemReady, to the hazard unit'\"'s\n  instruction-availability input. Preserve the existing load-use source\n  comparisons on the presented head instruction, ID/EX hold/bubble\n  priorities and all dmem/divider control. Thus a ready queued head can\n  advance while the external instruction bus stalls, and a blocked head\n  can accumulate additional predicted-path words while space remains.\n  When the queue is full and cannot dequeue, hold io_imemAddr stable.\n\n  Accepted EX recovery overrides all queue and bus activity: set fetch\n  PC to redirect_target and invalidate the entire queue on that edge,\n  with no enqueue/dequeue accepted. The existing ID/EX flush kills the\n  presented younger instruction. This also works when imemReady is zero\n  or the queue is full. Reset clears occupancy and pointers, resets PC\n  and predictor as before, and need not reset invalid queue payloads.\n  Wrong-path prefetches have no architectural effects and must never\n  retire, train, launch a divide, or access data memory. Preserve the\n  exact nret=1 ports, precise single retirement and +1 order sequence,\n  decoder legality, RV32M arithmetic/ALTOPS, memory bounds and handshakes.\n  This proposal changes front-end elasticity only: no cache, predictor\n  policy change, arithmetic redesign, or extra pipeline stage.\n\n  Expect a 7 percent net fitness gain with medium confidence, pending\n  measurements. Fetch-run-ahead may hide independent bus stalls during\n  existing execution bubbles, while empty fall-through avoids adding a\n  fixed recovery or instruction latency. A new head-selection mux and\n  wider predicted-path buffering can affect timing and wrong-path traffic;\n  the queue may also remain empty when fetch bandwidth is the bottleneck.\n  Measure actual canonical CoreMark cycles and all three Gowin options.\n  Require fitness above 142.69532 iterations/s, with the unchanged five\n  CRCs, UART success literal, in-bounds accesses and MMIO timing markers.\n  Run the existing unit, formal and stalled reference-cosimulation gates\n  without weakening bounds, assumptions or architectural assertions.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the four-entry fall-through circular instruction queue and\n      separate producer fetch-PC advancement from consumer acceptance.\n      Buffer PC, instruction and predicted_taken atomically; support\n      simultaneous enqueue/dequeue, full and empty transitions, and\n      reset/recovery invalidation. Keep predictor policy unchanged.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire queue head availability to the existing instruction hazard\n      input while retaining raw io_imemReady for the fetch producer.\n      Preserve top-level IO, ID/EX acceptance, backpressure and RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Document that the existing instruction-ready input now denotes\n      decode-head availability and stall_if blocks head consumption.\n      Retain load-use, divider, recovery and memory priority equations.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add queue fill/drain, simultaneous full pop/push, empty bypass,\n      long imem/dmem stalls, load-use and divide holds, wraparound,\n      redirects with a full queue or stalled bus, reset and dependent\n      branch/JALR cases. Check no lost/duplicate instructions, precise\n      RVFI PC/source data, retirement ordering and exactly-once stores.\n      Preserve architectural expectations; update only observations that\n      equated speculative io_imemAddr with the decode-head PC. Prove\n      that queued instructions drain during imem stalls and fetch can\n      run ahead during downstream holds, without changing the harness.\n''')\nprint('Replaced hypothesis with the fetch-queue proposal.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Replaced hypothesis with the fetch-queue proposal.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
