{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_71", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r9s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-002-r9s1\ntitle: \\\"Capture multiply operands and use EX/MEM as the product register\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 153.70 iterations/s at 61.131 MHz with 3,977,318\n  CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path runs from WB selection through\n  EX forwarding and the ALU into EX/MEM.alu_result. The prior r5s1\n  structural multiplier improved frequency but inserted two wait cycles\n  per multiply, increasing CoreMark cycles by 4.46 percent. A multiplier\n  operand boundary can remove forwarding from the arithmetic path while\n  using the existing EX/MEM register to avoid that extra completion cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the three combinational multiply expressions with one shared\n  signed 33-by-33 multiplier fed exclusively by captured operands. Use\n  a two-cycle blocking EX transaction: edge 1 captures the exact forwarded\n  operands and multiply opcode; edge 2 captures the selected product\n  directly in the existing EX/MEM.alu_result register and releases EX.\n  There is exactly ONE added wait cycle per multiply when MEM is ready.\n  Do not add a separate multiplier-result register or a third transfer\n  edge. Base ALU operations retain their existing latency and divider\n  arithmetic, latency and handshake remain unchanged.\n\n  In alu.sv add mul_req_valid/mul_req_ready and\n  mul_result_valid/mul_result_ready. A pending bit is zero when idle.\n  On request acceptance save 32-bit A, B and the opcode, and set pending.\n  While pending assert result_valid, deassert req_ready and keep operands\n  unchanged; clear pending only on result transfer or reset. The result\n  is combinational from these saved operands and stays stable under\n  backpressure. A's signed extension bit is saved A[31] only for MULH\n  and MULHSU; B's is saved B[31] only for MULH. Otherwise extend with\n  zero. Multiply explicit signed 33-bit operands into a signed 66-bit\n  product. Select bits 31:0 for MUL and bits 63:32 for MULH/MULHU/MULHSU.\n  Bits 65:64 are not part of the architectural high result. Remove the\n  old live-input products so the timed multiplier has no forwarding mux\n  before its operand registers. Keep opcode-dependent sign extension\n  and result selection driven by captured operation metadata.\n\n  In EX recognize only the four multiply operations. Launch once for\n  a valid multiply when \"'!stall and mul_req_ready. Assert execute_wait\n  for a valid multiply without result_valid, OR the existing divide\n  wait. Set result_ready only for the held valid multiply with !stall.\n  The existing hazard logic holds ID/EX and decode consumption while\n  older MEM/WB work drains and fetch can fill its queue. A dmem stall\n  keeps priority: preserve its EX/MEM request, then accept the multiply\n  only when that older transaction can advance. During the launch wait,\n  emit a fully cleared EX/MEM bubble rather than clearing valid alone.\n  On the following available edge, normal EX/MEM capture registers the\n  product and accepts the held decode successor. That successor receives\n  the product through ordinary EX/MEM or MEM/WB forwarding.\n\n  Save the original post-forwarding source values at multiply launch\n  for RVFI, sharing the existing divide operand-history registers since\n  blocking EX allows only one such operation at a time. Use saved values\n  at multiply retirement even if live forwarding producers have drained.\n  Keep multiply and divide transaction state independent; consecutive\n  multiplies and alternating MUL/DIV must neither relaunch nor consume\n  stale completion. Reset clears pending work. Wrong-path or invalid\n  instructions must not launch, retire or cause a memory side effect.\n  Preserve held MEM/WB forwarding, younger-writer priority, x0 behavior,\n  load-use bubbles, legality/traps, branch recovery and nret=1 IO.\n\n  Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS retain the SAME request edge, pending cycle\n  and earliest edge-2 transfer. Compute the existing MUL/MULH/MULHU\n  add-XOR and MULHSU subtract-XOR formulas from saved operands and opcode,\n  using the original constants. Only arithmetic changes under ALTOPS;\n  no formal-only latency shortcut is allowed. No change to wrappers,\n  formal bounds, benchmarks, memory contracts or correctness gates.\n\n  This single multiply-boundary change should trade roughly half the\n  earlier multiplier proposal'\"'s cycle penalty for a shorter global EX\n  path and fewer DSPs. Estimate ten percent net fitness improvement with\n  medium confidence; the exact multiply frequency, divider timing and\n  FPGA placement determine the result. Measure actual bracketed cycles\n  and all three vendor placement options. Acceptance requires greater\n  than 160.7702 iterations/s, not a faster single placement option.\n\n  Validate all real and ALTOPS arithmetic vectors with the new handshake,\n  captured operands, held completion, reset cancellation, consecutive\n  requests and every signed high-product corner. Test forwarded multiply\n  inputs from ALU/load/WB, immediate consumers, same-rd writers, x0,\n  dependent branches, back-to-back multiplies and alternating divides,\n  plus older stores and arbitrary bus stalls. Check once-only retirement,\n  exact RVFI source/result data, contiguous order and memory effects.\n  Run unchanged formal and stalled reference cosimulation, canonical\n  CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation and median Gowin timing.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the captured-operand multiply request/result handshake and\n      one pending bit. Replace live-input products with one signed\n      33-by-33 shared product from saved operands/opcode. Present the\n      selected result combinationally while pending; EX/MEM supplies\n      the product register. Preserve base ALU/divider behavior and\n      use identical two-edge control timing for ALTOPS formulas.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add multiply launch/completion wiring and extend execute_wait.\n      Capture once, transfer directly to EX/MEM on the next available\n      edge, and reuse saved divide-source metadata for multiply RVFI.\n      Preserve dmem priority, fully cleared wait bubbles, forwarding,\n      redirect acceptance and the divider's existing transaction.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt multiply checks to request capture and earliest next-edge\n      consumption while retaining every existing reference vector and\n      assertion. Keep base operations combinational and divider latency\n      unchanged. Test all high-product sign combinations and extrema,\n      changed live operands/opcode while pending, held completion,\n      reset cancellation, consecutive requests and ALTOPS timing parity.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all current architectural checks; add multiply forwarding\n      and immediate-consumer cases, back-to-back MUL and alternating\n      MUL/DIV, original RVFI source capture, x0, same-rd writers, reset,\n      wrong-path suppression and long bus stalls around launch/transfer.\n      Check one added wait cycle when ready, exactly-once retirement,\n      contiguous RVFI order and older memory transactions occurring once.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r9s1.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised structural hypothesis is schema-valid.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
