{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d['title'],d.get('outcome'),d.get('fitness'),d.get('cycles'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  if isinstance(h,dict):\n   print(h.get('motivation',''))\n   if d['id'].endswith(('r6s2','r7s2','r8s2')): print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 Compact bimodal branch prediction with immediate direct targets regression 13.53 4201709\nThe baseline fetches sequentially and redirects every taken branch and jump\nfrom EX. Although described as five stages, IF/ID is combinational, so each\nsuch redirect discards one younger fetch slot. Repeated loops and direct\njumps in CoreMark therefore incur avoidable bubbles on top of the roughly\n22 percent bus stalls. The sole recorded experiment is the baseline; there\nare no prior predictor attempts or LESSONS.md. Its reported critical path\ncrosses forwarding and the combinational ALU, so a small predictor can\nimprove iterations per cycle without attempting to change that arithmetic\npath. Any frequency benefit is excluded from the expected gain.\n\n\n hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 Apply validated bimodal prediction to the clocked-divider champion regression 77.22 4201709\nThe current champion still fetches sequentially and flushes on every\ntaken branch or jump. IF/ID is combinational, so each EX redirect wastes\none younger fetch slot, rather than the two implied by the stage names.\nA predictor can remove this bubble without changing the arithmetic path.\n\nLESSONS.md identifies the clocked divider as the source of the champion's\nclock improvement; retain that implementation. The relevant predictor\nexperiment, hyp-20261001-003-r1s2, passed formal and cosimulation and cut\nbracketed CoreMark cycles from 4,491,485 to 4,201,709, a 6.45 percent\nreduction. Its regression label compares its 13.53 score against the\ndivider champion: its own critical path still crossed the old\ncombinational ALU, and its Fmax was only 5.685 MHz. This is evidence of a\nuseful predictor on the older parent, not a measurement of prediction\ncombined with the current 31.675 MHz clocked-divider parent. The current\nchampion also records 4,491,485 cycles, making this combination the most\ndirectly supported predictor experiment.\n\n\n hyp-20261001-003-r3s2 Combine proven bimodal prediction with the six-step-divider champion regression 94.47 4201709\nThe \n…[20160 of 23232 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:853c2ca979ad4e34]…\n03-r9s2 Persistent bimodal predictor with registered EX training improvement 161.21 3977318\nThe champion already predicts direct BRANCH/JAL and buffers four fetches.\nPrior profiling found only about 52,000 conditional misses; fresh trace\nreplay counts just 13,735 returns, so larger direction tables and return\nprediction have limited cycle headroom. LESSONS.md records clock gains\nfrom removing predictor reset logic on older parents, but carrying direct\ntargets regressed and MEM-stage verification failed liveness.\n\nOn this exact parent, placement-option-1 probes measured 59.318 MHz for\npersistent counters alone and 61.346 MHz for registered training alone.\nCombining persistent counters with registered training measured 64.119 MHz\nin the same vendor flow. The former exposes WB/EX-to-counter training as\na worst path; registering that event isolates counter updates while the\npersistent bank removes reset-related logic. This is one predictor\nimplementation change with complementary parts, not an accuracy redesign.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
