{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"agent_message","text":"The storage changes have not produced a useful clock gain. I’m refining the hypothesis around load-dependent branches: let prediction carry them past the load-use bubble and verify them in MEM. All other branches would still resolve in EX, avoiding the blanket delay used by the earlier proposal that failed liveness."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_52", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r10s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-003-r10s2',\n'title':'Selectively verify load-dependent predicted branches in MEM',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The existing predictor and fetch queue already remove most direct-control bubbles. Prior profiling reports roughly 52,000 conditional misses and 13,735 returns, so improving accuracy alone offers little headroom. Current-parent probes of counter rewrites and split lookup/training storage reduced area in some cases but did not establish the required clock gain. The remaining promising predictor angle is hiding branch operand latency.\n\nLESSONS.md records a 3.26 percent cycle saving from eliminating load-to-branch interlocks, but direct memory-to-EX forwarding lost Fmax. The r8s2 proposal moved every conditional branch's verification to MEM and failed the unchanged liveness check. Selective verification can target the load-use bubbles without imposing that extra recovery cycle on independent branches.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Keep the existing 64-entry persistent bimodal predictor, registered training stage and four-entry fetch queue. Remove the load-use interlock only for a positively decoded legal conditional branch whose source depends on the immediately preceding valid load's nonzero destination. Issue this branch using its captured prediction, defer its operand verification to MEM, and obtain the load result from the existing registered WB path. All other conditional branches and all JAL/JALR continue resolving in EX. This is one predictor integration change, not a new direction algorithm or arithmetic redesign.\n\nIdentify the exceptional branch in EX by matching either source against the immediately older EX/MEM load, with valid, mem_read and rd\"'!=0 qualification. Carry a deferred-branch bit, two operand-repair flags and the original prediction in EX/MEM. Reuse its existing full-width branch_target, PC, source addresses and source values. A branch immediately behind a load may advance early only because this deferred path will verify it; ordinary load-use interlocks remain for all nonbranches and reserved branch encodings. Data-bus backpressure still holds the load and following branch until the load is accepted.\n\nFor each matched operand, retain the value that would exist if the older load does not write: prefer a matching retained MEM/WB writer, otherwise use the captured ID/EX value. Exclude the pending EX/MEM load'\"'s alu_result, which is its address, from this fallback. In MEM, replace only marked operands with WB data when the immediately older load actually writes that matching nonzero destination. A trapping misaligned load suppresses its write, so the fallback is required. Repair both sources independently, including the case where both read the same destination. Unmatched operands keep normal forwarded EX values.\n\nFor a deferred branch only, suppress EX condition-based redirect, training and target-fault decisions. In MEM compute the six architectural branch conditions from the repaired operands, check the registered target's alignment and resolve pc_next and traps exactly as the original EX path did. Report the repaired source values through RVFI. A not-taken branch with an unaligned encoded target remains legal; an actually taken misaligned branch traps and continues at PC+4. Nondeferred instructions keep the existing MEM behavior.\n\nA deferred MEM misprediction takes priority over every younger EX action. Retire the older branch normally, redirect IF, discard the complete fetch queue, flush ID/EX, and clear EX/MEM instead of capturing the younger instruction. Suppress younger divider requests, EX redirects and training on that edge. Recovery must beat a wrong-path divide's execute_wait; arrange kill priority explicitly before hold/accept decisions. The resolving MEM branch has no data-memory operation, so unrelated io_dmemReady cannot stall it. Correctly predicted deferred branches permit the younger instruction to advance normally. Preserve immediate EX recovery for all ordinary branches and jumps.\n\nMEM and EX can both offer branch training on a correctly predicted deferred branch followed by another conditional branch. Define a simple, explicit arbitration policy: the accepted legal nonfaulting MEM event has priority; if an accepted EX event coincides, discard only that younger learning event. A squashed EX branch never trains. This advisory drop changes prediction quality only and avoids adding a two-write table, training queue or execution stall. Train all noncolliding accepted eligible branches once, retaining the current one-cycle registered training stage and live-entry saturation. Test the intentional collision behavior rather than assuming every accepted branch always trains.\n\nThis selective exception addresses the earlier liveness failure structurally. In a zero-wait load/branch pair, the new branch verifies in MEM on the same edge where the original interlocked branch would have verified in EX. A correct prediction saves the bubble; a misprediction does not acquire the extra recovery cycle imposed by moving every branch to MEM. Independent conditional branches keep their original recovery timing. This timing argument is not a proof: unchanged liveness, register-consistency, instruction, trap and uniqueness checks must all pass without changing formal depth or assumptions.\n\nThe prior 3.26 percent cycle saving is evidence of available load-branch bubbles on an older parent, not a measured result for this proposal. Estimate six percent net fitness improvement with low confidence: roughly three percent fewer cycles plus a modest frequency benefit from keeping data-memory output out of EX comparison. At the historical 3.26 percent cycle saving, passing the current 168.62566 iterations/s threshold needs approximately 64.88 MHz, versus the champion's 64.119 MHz. Added repair muxes, recovery fanout, learning collisions and the still-critical divider may prevent this gain. No implementation, correctness or timing result is claimed for this selective design.\n\nPreserve nret=1, every top-level port, decoder default-illegal behavior, full addresses, precise RVFI order, existing forwarding retention, all memory handshakes, divider/ALTOPS and canonical evaluation contracts. Validate all six conditions, either/both dependent sources, prolonged independent bus stalls, trapping loads and fallback writers, both prediction errors, full-queue recovery, reset and misaligned targets. Put stores, loads, DIV/REM, JALR and another branch immediately after a deferred branch to check age-priority recovery and training collisions. Check cycle savings on warm correctly predicted load-branch loops and unchanged independent-branch recovery latency. Run the existing unit suite, unchanged formal and stalled reference cosimulation, then canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation and median Gowin timing.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'''Positively identify legal conditional branches at the decode head and exempt only those from an otherwise asserted load-use interlock. Preserve every nonbranch interlock and bus hold. Ensure older MEM branch recovery flushes younger execution even when a wrong-path divide would otherwise hold ID/EX.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add only branch_deferred, two deferred-load source flags and predicted_taken to ex_mem_t. Reuse existing branch target, PC and operand fields; leave external RVFI shape and downstream architectural fields intact.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Detect a legal branch's dependency on the immediately older valid EX/MEM load. Capture fallback operands and deferred metadata, bypassing verification only for that case. Keep ordinary branch/jump resolution in EX. Add an older-recovery kill with priority over holds; suppress killed divider launch, acceptance, redirects and learning. Preserve normal ALU, divide and memory datapaths.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Verify only deferred branches using marked operands repaired from matching WB writes, with correct trapping-load fallbacks. Produce accepted MEM recovery and training events, resolved RVFI operands, next PC and trap state. Preserve all existing load/store formatting, alignment, bus and WB-retention behavior.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Provide decode-head instruction qualification and WB fallback/repair metadata. Merge MEM and EX recovery by age, wire the EX kill and IF/ID flush, and arbitrate the existing single training input with eligible MEM learning ahead of EX learning. On a simultaneous correct-path collision intentionally drop only the younger EX learning event; never stall execution for predictor updates. Keep the persistent IF table and registered update implementation unchanged.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Extend predictor observation for selective MEM verification and explicit MEM-priority learning collisions, retaining every architectural assertion. Add warm dependent-branch cycle checks, independent EX-resolution checks, all conditions and operand dependencies, stalls, reset, trapping-load fallback, target faults, full-queue recovery and wrong-path store/divide/jump suppression. Include load/branch/divide and consecutive branch sequences relevant to bounded liveness; never relax the unchanged formal liveness gate.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=92))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()));print('Revised hypothesis is schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
