{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r12s2.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-003-r12s2',\n'title':'Register prediction recovery and annul the younger EX instruction',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 199.08 iterations/s at 79.422 MHz and 3,989,539 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path starts at an ID/EX branch operand and reaches the IF PC. Recent recovery-bank and narrow-squash work did not remove the late forwarded comparison from fetch control. Local same-latency probes also failed acceptance: a preselected ID correction address reached only 69.09 MHz median, while balanced/factored comparator variants remained below the champion. The next useful predictor angle is to put an actual register boundary between verification and frontend recovery.\n\nEarlier r8s2 moved every conditional comparison to MEM, removed load-branch interlocks and failed liveness. This proposal keeps comparison, architectural EX advancement, load-use interlocks and training at their existing points. It delays only the correction event and target, with explicit younger-EX annulment. The unchanged liveness bound remains mandatory; a clock gain cannot excuse a failure.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Pipeline only the predictor recovery event. Keep the existing 64-entry persistent bimodal predictor, direct BRANCH/JAL predictions, original forwarded EX comparisons, load-use interlocks, EX architectural result capture and delayed counter-training path. EX produces its existing accepted misprediction/JALR event and a full-width correction candidate. Register the event for exactly one clock, clearing it on reset and capturing false on every cycle without an accepted event. Capture the correction candidate unconditionally in a separate 32-bit register. The following cycle's registered event, not the live EX condition, drives the existing IF redirect/target and hazard flush inputs.\n\nRemove the late branch outcome from correction-address data selection. For direct control transfers select PC+4 when the original captured predicted_taken bit is one, otherwise PC+immediate. On a direct prediction error this is precisely the required alternative successor. For JALR select the existing full forwarded (rs1+immediate)&~1 target using the registered instruction class. Do not change architectural pc_next, target-fault detection, JAL/JALR link values or actual_taken. The correction address need only match the architectural successor when the accepted event is true; unconditional candidate capture must not become a redirect-qualified wide register enable or mux. A false event leaves its payload irrelevant.\n\nAt the EX event edge the resolving older control transfer advances normally into EX/MEM, while one younger instruction may enter ID/EX. During the next cycle, the registered recovery event annuls that younger EX instruction before any effect: suppress EX acceptance, new recovery, branch training, multiplier/divider request-valid and result-ready, and execute_wait. On the recovery edge inject a completely zero EX/MEM bubble, with annul taking priority over ordinary EX/MEM hold or execute wait. Preserve all forwarding and arithmetic logic when no annul is active. A wrong-path load/store must never reach EX/MEM, and a wrong-path M instruction must never launch. No already-started M operation can coexist with an older accepted control-transfer event in this blocking in-order pipeline; do not introduce cancellation of legitimate arithmetic.\n\nUse the same registered event to discard the complete fetch queue, select the registered target into the original IF PC register and flush ID/EX. Recovery retains priority over instruction-bus readiness, queue occupancy and younger execution wait. The resolving older branch/jump is now in MEM and retires normally; it has no data-memory transaction. Therefore arbitrary io_dmemReady must not hold recovery, and older retirement/order must not be suppressed. Reset discards any pending event. Consecutive events must not be replayed: the annulled younger instruction cannot generate a new event or training, and the event register captures false while recovery is consumed.\n\nKeep IF's single PC register and four-entry queue intact, including its empty bypass and existing payload-write guards. Add no alternate-PC bank, target cache, return stack, direction-table change, speculative history or data-path optimization. Architectural branch comparison and RVFI results stay in EX; only control recovery crosses the new one-cycle boundary. Correct predictions retain their exact existing timing. Each actual correction costs one extra recovery cycle; the hypothesis is that clock improvement outweighs this small additional bubble rate. Preserve nret=1, all top-level ports, decoder default-illegal discipline, full addresses, target traps, precise RVFI order and all memory handshakes.\n\nExpect about seven percent net fitness improvement with low confidence pending measurement. Historical counts near 52,000 conditional misses plus 13,735 JALRs suggest approximately 1.6 percent extra cycles, but delayed training/fetch interaction can change predictions, so remeasure the canonical workload rather than fixing its cycle count. At a 1.6 percent cycle penalty, acceptance requires roughly 84.4 MHz instead of the champion's 79.422 MHz. The acceptance threshold remains above 208.23768 iterations/s. Remaining comparator-to-event, JALR-adder-to-target, general ALU or divider paths may prevent this improvement, and the fixed formal liveness bound is a concrete risk.\n\nRun the unchanged formal suite before claiming a viable candidate, especially liveness, pc_fwd, reg, unique and all branch/jump checks. Do not change assumptions, depth, arithmetic latency, wrappers or gates to make delayed recovery pass. Test both prediction errors, all six predicates, back-to-back branches/jumps, load/ALU/WB-forwarded conditions and JALR, full queues, independent long bus stalls, reset with a pending event, and target-alignment traps. Put STORE, LOAD, DIV/REM, MUL, JALR and another branch immediately after a misprediction; assert zero wrong-path requests, EX acceptance, training, register writes and retirements. Check exactly one event, one added recovery cycle and the exact architectural RVFI sequence. Preserve every existing architectural assertion while updating implementation-specific observations to distinguish the EX event from its registered IF application. Finish with stalled reference cosimulation, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/markers and all scored Gowin placement options.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Separate the live EX recovery event/target from the IF recovery wires. Add one reset-cleared event register and one unconditionally captured full-width target register. Feed the registered pair to existing IF/hazard recovery ports and feed the registered event to a new EX annul input. Keep all external and RVFI interfaces and the training wires unchanged.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Add a recovery-annul input. Gate execute_wait, EX acceptance, training/redirect through acceptance, and all multiply/divide request/completion handshakes with \"'!annul. Give annul priority to write a completely zero EX/MEM bubble. Select direct correction candidates from the original prediction (taken -> PC+4, otherwise direct target) and JALR candidates from the masked forwarded sum, independently of branch outcome. Preserve all existing architectural predicates, operands, targets, faults, pc_next, links and normal execution behavior.'\"'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Model the live accepted EX recovery event separately from the next-cycle IF application and exclude annulled EX instructions from acceptance/training expectations. Update only assertions that encode the original recovery latency; retain every architectural and side-effect check. Add directed pending-event/reset, full-queue and independent bus-stall cases, plus a wrong-path load/store/M operation/jump/branch in the younger EX slot. Verify one extra cycle per correction, no extra cycle on hits, zero wrong-path requests or effects, correct older retirement, exact RVFI PCs/operands/orders/traps and once-only training. Keep formal depth and assumptions unchanged.'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=92))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated schema-valid hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r12s2.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-003-r12s2',\n'title':'Register prediction recovery and annul the younger EX instruction',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 199.08 iterations/s at 79.422 MHz and 3,989,539 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path starts at an ID/EX branch operand and reaches the IF PC. Recent recovery-bank and narrow-squash work did not remove the late forwarded comparison from fetch control. Local same-latency probes also failed acceptance: a preselected ID correction address reached only 69.09 MHz median, while balanced/factored comparator variants remained below the champion. The next useful predictor angle is to put an actual register boundary between verification and frontend recovery.\n\nEarlier r8s2 moved every conditional comparison to MEM, removed load-branch interlocks and failed liveness. This proposal keeps comparison, architectural EX advancement, load-use interlocks and training at their existing points. It delays only the correction event and target, with explicit younger-EX annulment. The unchanged liveness bound remains mandatory; a clock gain cannot excuse a failure.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Pipeline only the predictor recovery event. Keep the existing 64-entry persistent bimodal predictor, direct BRANCH/JAL predictions, original forwarded EX comparisons, load-use interlocks, EX architectural result capture and delayed counter-training path. EX produces its existing accepted misprediction/JALR event and a full-width correction candidate. Register the event for exactly one clock, clearing it on reset and capturing false on every cycle without an accepted event. Capture the correction candidate unconditionally in a separate 32-bit register. The following cycle's registered event, not the live EX condition, drives the existing IF redirect/target and hazard flush inputs.\n\nRemove the late branch outcome from correction-address data selection. For direct control transfers select PC+4 when the original captured predicted_taken bit is one, otherwise PC+immediate. On a direct prediction error this is precisely the required alternative successor. For JALR select the existing full forwarded (rs1+immediate)&~1 target using the registered instruction class. Do not change architectural pc_next, target-fault detection, JAL/JALR link values or actual_taken. The correction address need only match the architectural successor when the accepted event is true; unconditional candidate capture must not become a redirect-qualified wide register enable or mux. A false event leaves its payload irrelevant.\n\nAt the EX event edge the resolving older control transfer advances normally into EX/MEM, while one younger instruction may enter ID/EX. During the next cycle, the registered recovery event annuls that younger EX instruction before any effect: suppress EX acceptance, new recovery, branch training, multiplier/divider request-valid and result-ready, and execute_wait. On the recovery edge inject a completely zero EX/MEM bubble, with annul taking priority over ordinary EX/MEM hold or execute wait. Preserve all forwarding and arithmetic logic when no annul is active. A wrong-path load/store must never reach EX/MEM, and a wrong-path M instruction must never launch. No already-started M operation can coexist with an older accepted control-transfer event in this blocking in-order pipeline; do not introduce cancellation of legitimate arithmetic.\n\nUse the same registered event to discard the complete fetch queue, select the registered target into the original IF PC register and flush ID/EX. Recovery retains priority over instruction-bus readiness, queue occupancy and younger execution wait. The resolving older branch/jump is now in MEM and retires normally; it has no data-memory transaction. Therefore arbitrary io_dmemReady must not hold recovery, and older retirement/order must not be suppressed. Reset discards any pending event. Consecutive events must not be replayed: the annulled younger instruction cannot generate a new event or training, and the event register captures false while recovery is consumed.\n\nKeep IF's single PC register and four-entry queue intact, including its empty bypass and existing payload-write guards. Add no alternate-PC bank, target cache, return stack, direction-table change, speculative history or data-path optimization. Architectural branch comparison and RVFI results stay in EX; only control recovery crosses the new one-cycle boundary. Correct predictions retain their exact existing timing. Each actual correction costs one extra recovery cycle; the hypothesis is that clock improvement outweighs this small additional bubble rate. Preserve nret=1, all top-level ports, decoder default-illegal discipline, full addresses, target traps, precise RVFI order and all memory handshakes.\n\nExpect about seven percent net fitness improvement with low confidence pending measurement. Historical counts near 52,000 conditional misses plus 13,735 JALRs suggest approximately 1.6 percent extra cycles, but delayed training/fetch interaction can change predictions, so remeasure the canonical workload rather than fixing its cycle count. At a 1.6 percent cycle penalty, acceptance requires roughly 84.4 MHz instead of the champion's 79.422 MHz. The acceptance threshold remains above 208.23768 iterations/s. Remaining comparator-to-event, JALR-adder-to-target, general ALU or divider paths may prevent this improvement, and the fixed formal liveness bound is a concrete risk.\n\nRun the unchanged formal suite before claiming a viable candidate, especially liveness, pc_fwd, reg, unique and all branch/jump checks. Do not change assumptions, depth, arithmetic latency, wrappers or gates to make delayed recovery pass. Test both prediction errors, all six predicates, back-to-back branches/jumps, load/ALU/WB-forwarded conditions and JALR, full queues, independent long bus stalls, reset with a pending event, and target-alignment traps. Put STORE, LOAD, DIV/REM, MUL, JALR and another branch immediately after a misprediction; assert zero wrong-path requests, EX acceptance, training, register writes and retirements. Check exactly one event, one added recovery cycle and the exact architectural RVFI sequence. Preserve every existing architectural assertion while updating implementation-specific observations to distinguish the EX event from its registered IF application. Finish with stalled reference cosimulation, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/markers and all scored Gowin placement options.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Separate the live EX recovery event/target from the IF recovery wires. Add one reset-cleared event register and one unconditionally captured full-width target register. Feed the registered pair to existing IF/hazard recovery ports and feed the registered event to a new EX annul input. Keep all external and RVFI interfaces and the training wires unchanged.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Add a recovery-annul input. Gate execute_wait, EX acceptance, training/redirect through acceptance, and all multiply/divide request/completion handshakes with \"'!annul. Give annul priority to write a completely zero EX/MEM bubble. Select direct correction candidates from the original prediction (taken -> PC+4, otherwise direct target) and JALR candidates from the masked forwarded sum, independently of branch outcome. Preserve all existing architectural predicates, operands, targets, faults, pc_next, links and normal execution behavior.'\"'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Model the live accepted EX recovery event separately from the next-cycle IF application and exclude annulled EX instructions from acceptance/training expectations. Update only assertions that encode the original recovery latency; retain every architectural and side-effect check. Add directed pending-event/reset, full-queue and independent bus-stall cases, plus a wrong-path load/store/M operation/jump/branch in the younger EX slot. Verify one extra cycle per correction, no extra cycle on hits, zero wrong-path requests or effects, correct older retirement, exact RVFI PCs/operands/orders/traps and once-only training. Keep formal depth and assumptions unchanged.'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=92))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated schema-valid hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Updated schema-valid hypothesis\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
