{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"agent_message","text":"The local timing probes did not clear the acceptance margin. The useful finding is that holding the resolving instruction through recovery passed all 53 unchanged formal checks in the registered-EX prototype. I’ll base the final hypothesis on that interlock with MEM-stage prediction verification, where load-dependent branches can use registered WB data. That combined design remains unmeasured; the YAML will state that explicitly."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r14s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-003-r14s2',\n'title':'MEM-stage prediction verification with recovery-before-retirement interlock',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 211.52 iterations/s at 84.388 MHz and 3,989,539 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path runs from registered WB data through EX prediction verification to fetch-queue payload control. Larger direction tables offer limited headroom: the prior retirement-order model recorded only about 51,419 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs. Target the location of prediction verification rather than predictor capacity.\n\nPrior r8s2 moved conditional verification to MEM and failed liveness; r10s2 and r12s1 deferred only load-dependent branches and retained an ordinary EX-to-IF recovery path plus age arbitration. The proposed single redesign verifies every control transfer at MEM, and retains a correcting instruction until the fetch correction has been applied. This preserves a bounded retirement gap while allowing conditional branches to consume a preceding load's registered WB value without an EX load-use bubble.\n\nA disposable probe during this proposal registered EX recovery and held EX/MEM plus WB advancement for its correction cycle. It passed all 53 unchanged fast formal checks, including liveness, and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation. It measured 4,055,160 bracketed cycles and 84.150 MHz for placement option 1, so that probe alone does NOT meet acceptance. This establishes evidence for the recovery interlock, not performance or correctness of the final MEM-verification design. Other local EX-only verification simplifications also failed to show sufficient timing gains.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Move prediction verification for all BRANCH, JAL and JALR instructions to the existing MEM stage and introduce one recovery-before-retirement interlock for a mispredicted control transfer. This is one predictor-resolution architecture. Retain the current 64 persistent bimodal counters, registered training-event update, direct-target fetch prediction, four-entry fall-through queue, arithmetic units and instruction latencies. No predictor-size sweep, cache, return stack or arithmetic redesign is proposed.\n\nEX prepares control-transfer packets but does not generate redirects or conditional training. Carry the original predicted_taken, the four predecoded branch predicate bits, and two deferred-load operand flags in EX/MEM. Reuse its PC, instruction, source identities, source values and direct target; retain the existing full forwarded JALR sum with bit zero cleared. Capture an unambiguous resolved jump target, either in the existing jump pc_next payload or a dedicated field. Preserve JAL/JALR link PC+4 and their target-alignment write suppression. Conditional outcome, conditional target fault and final architectural conditional pc_next are determined only in MEM. Remove the now-unused EX conditional comparator from timed logic.\n\nFor a conditional branch whose source matches the valid immediately older EX/MEM load's nonzero rd, mark that operand deferred. Do not capture the load's EX/MEM arithmetic/address result as its branch operand. Capture a fallback value selected from a matching retained MEM/WB register writer, otherwise the original ID/EX operand. Other branch operands use ordinary EX forwarding. In MEM, replace a deferred operand with registered MEM/WB.wb_data only when that immediately older producer actually has reg_write set and its nonzero rd matches. Check retained control and identity, not rvfi_valid or the valid-gated regfile write pulse: WB data/control remains a legitimate source through a hold. If the older load trapped and suppressed its register write, use the saved fallback. Apply repair independently to both sources and report the repaired values in branch RVFI.\n\nSuppress the load-use bubble only for a positively decoded legal conditional-branch head with funct3 in {0,1,4,5,6,7}. Its comparison now occurs after the load data has entered WB. Keep every other load-use interlock, including JALR, and retain the existing data-bus backpressure: an unready older memory operation prevents the branch from leaving EX. Do not add a combinational memory-data-to-branch path; repair exclusively from registered WB data.\n\nMEM compares the saved/repaired operands using the champion's balanced native two-bit leaves and captured predicate controls. Compute the actual legal aligned transfer, architectural resolved next PC, target fault and prediction mismatch. A taken misaligned branch traps, suppresses its transfer and resolves architecturally to PC+4; a not-taken branch with a misaligned encoded target remains legal. Direct JAL is checked against its captured prediction; an aligned legal JALR remains an unpredicted transfer. Illegal instructions neither redirect nor train. For direct control, correction is the alternative to the captured prediction, predicted_taken ? PC+4 : saved direct target; JALR uses its saved masked target. Never substitute this alternative address for resolved RVFI pc_next when no correction is needed.\n\nUse MEM as the sole recovery producer. Eliminate EX-vs-MEM redirect arbitration entirely. A valid control packet has no data-memory transaction, so unrelated io_dmemReady must not delay its verification. Correct predictions advance normally to WB and permit the younger EX instruction to advance in the same cycle. Train a legal, non-faulting conditional branch exactly once when it actually advances from MEM to WB, with its real condition and its own PC[7:2]. Route this MEM event through the existing IF training-event register. A correcting branch trains on its final advancement, not on both verification cycles.\n\nDefine a one-bit recovery_applied_q, reset to zero. On the first MEM mismatch for the held control packet, assert correction = mismatch && \"'!recovery_applied_q. On that edge: update the IF PC and invalidate the entire fetch queue; flush ID/EX with priority over any younger execution wait; hold the resolving EX/MEM packet; and clear MEM/WB.valid while retaining all of its data/control for forwarding. Set recovery_applied_q. Suppress the younger EX instruction'\"'s multiply/divide requests and EX/MEM acceptance on this correction cycle. No wrong-path load or store may enter the memory stage. Because the resolving packet is a control transfer, holding it cannot duplicate a data-bus transaction.\n\nOn the following cycle the same EX/MEM control packet remains present, but recovery_applied_q suppresses repeated correction. Advance that packet to WB exactly once, clear recovery_applied_q when it leaves, and allow EX/MEM to take the flushed younger bubble. The corrected instruction can now enter ID/EX. MEM/WB retained the older producer through the correction edge, so any deferred branch source stays stable until this final advancement. Reset cancels the interlock. If any additional internal hold is introduced, retain recovery_applied_q and the packet until the actual advancement; never clear the bit merely because a clock elapsed while holding the same packet.\n\nThe correcting instruction therefore remains an actual pipeline resident until after its correction is applied. Its link write, if any, and its sole RVFI retirement occur together at the real WB boundary. This is not an RVFI-only delay or a fabricated retirement. It delays both the correction path and the correcting instruction's retirement relative to EX verification, so the gap from that retirement to a slow instruction on the corrected path need not grow as in the failed r8s2 design. The existing liveness proof must still pass unchanged for the final design; the EX-interlock probe is evidence, not a substitute for that proof.\n\nMaintain the normal MEM load/store implementation and WB payload-retention semantics. Give recovery hold higher priority than a younger EX capture, and recovery squash higher priority than a younger execute_wait. The resolving packet has no memory request, so an apparent data stall from the wrong-path EX instruction cannot block correction. Forwarding metadata must reflect the actual held EX/MEM and retained WB registers on the interlock edge and refresh normally afterward. Preserve youngest-writer priority, x0, complete inert bubble controls, full-width addresses, decoder default-illegal behavior, nret=1 and every top-level IO/RVFI port. Leave multiplier/divider arithmetic, ALTOPS schedules and all external contracts unchanged.\n\nThe estimated fitness gain is six percent with low confidence. Load-dependent branches can save the roughly 2.7 percent cycle opportunity seen in earlier experiments, while one extra recovery cycle per miss/JALR consumes part of that saving. The final delayed training schedule and stalls require fresh measurement; no final cycle count is asserted. Removing EX comparison and all EX recovery arbitration may improve both branch timing and loading on the shared EX operand network. Competing general-ALU/divider paths, the new WB-to-MEM comparison path, and added timed operand registers remain material risks. Acceptance requires more than 221.24992 iterations/s using the measured bracketed cycles and median of all scored Gowin placements. No final implementation, median timing result or accepted gain is claimed.\n\nValidate the two-phase interlock first with the unchanged liveness, unique, reg, pc_fwd and pc_bwd checks, followed by the full unchanged fast formal suite. Add directed branch-recovery-then-DIV/REM cases, including JALR recovery followed by blocking arithmetic. Exercise every branch condition, both repaired operands, a trapping preceding load and fallback to a retained WB writer. Place stores, loads, jumps and MUL/DIV immediately behind a misprediction and prove that no younger request, register write, retirement or training survives correction. Check that a pending link instruction writes once at its delayed architectural retirement and that target instructions see its forwarded link correctly.\n\nAdapt only microarchitectural timing observers to MEM verification and the explicit interlock; retain all architectural assertions and formal bounds. Verify one correction, one eventual retirement and one training event per eligible packet, complete queue invalidation even when full or imem unready, correct register holds on both interlock edges, reset cancellation, contiguous RVFI order, repaired RVFI operands and exact resolved PCs/traps. Run stalled reference cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks, then all scored vendor placement options. The prior EX-interlock probe's unchanged pipeline observers expected EX-cycle training/recovery and therefore did not all pass; those observations must be updated to the specified MEM schedule without weakening correctness checks.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Extend EX/MEM with the original prediction, captured branch predicates, deferred-load operand flags and any required saved jump target. Preserve all architectural fields and the IF packet layout.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Prepare branch operands and fault-safe deferred-load fallbacks, carry prediction and targets into MEM, and remove EX conditional verification/training and all EX redirects. Preserve jump target/link computation. Add an older-recovery kill/hold input that retains the resolving EX/MEM packet and suppresses younger arithmetic requests and acceptance.'},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Repair conditional operands from registered retained WB data, verify all control transfers, resolve architectural PC/traps, and supply sole recovery/training events. Implement recovery_applied_q and the first-correction WB-valid bubble followed by exactly-once advancement of the held control packet. Preserve ordinary memory and WB retention behavior.'},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Exempt only legal conditional-branch heads from the ordinary EX load-use bubble. Give MEM correction priority over younger holds, hold EX/MEM for the resolving packet, clear MEM/WB valid while retaining payload, and flush ID/EX. Preserve all other interlocks and bus backpressure.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire sole MEM recovery/training, registered WB repair data and retained producer eligibility, pending-load identity, and the recovery hold/younger-kill protocol. Remove obsolete EX recovery wiring. Preserve nret=1 and the complete external interface.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Model MEM verification and recovery-before-retirement explicitly while preserving architectural checks. Add load-branch repair/fault fallback, mismatch-to-DIV liveness schedules, delayed link forwarding, exactly-once correction/training/retirement, reset, full-queue stalls and wrong-path memory/arithmetic suppression cases. Keep all formal bounds unchanged.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=95))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261002-003-r14s2' and d['category']=='predictor'\nprint('Wrote and schema-validated',p)\nprint('Bytes:',p.stat().st_size)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
