{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"agent_message","text":"The best EX-only probe reached 85.416 MHz, below the acceptance requirement. I’m revising the hypothesis around the r14s2 MEM verifier’s reported bottleneck: remove recovery from forwarding-metadata updates when the younger instruction is being flushed, and resolve load-forwarding eligibility before MEM. This final design is unmeasured; I’ll mark it low confidence."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r15s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-003-r15s2',\n'title':'MEM prediction verification with recovery isolated from forwarding metadata',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 211.52 iterations/s at 84.388 MHz and 3,989,539 bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md and the predictor experiments establish limited accuracy-only headroom: a prior retirement-order model estimated 51,419 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs. The opportunity is prediction verification and recovery timing.\n\nThe r14s2 MEM verifier passed formal and cosimulation and reduced measured cycles to 3,937,748, but scored only 182.44 at 71.840 MHz. Its worst path ran from the resolving branch's saved operand through MEM recovery and EX control to u_fwd/rs1_ex_match_q. Recovery was allowed to control forwarding metadata for a younger consumer that recovery simultaneously destroys. That is a removable dependency, not a necessary part of branch verification. Its MEM operand repair also rechecked the identity and write eligibility of an immediately older load whose relationship can be established before the EX/MEM edge.\n\nFresh disposable EX-only probes during this proposal did not establish the acceptance margin. Seven parallel comparison lanes on the current parent reached 85.416 MHz on placement option 1 and retained exactly 3,989,539 validated CoreMark cycles. Other predicate/recovery formulations and a smaller three-lane comparison were slower on that placement. The seven-lane probe passed both pipeline-test configurations after updating packed-field observers, but its clock result remains below the roughly 88.27 MHz required at unchanged cycles. Therefore the final proposal instead targets the specific avoidable feedback in MEM verification; it does not combine these comparator experiments with another category.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Move all control-transfer verification to MEM with the recovery-before-retirement interlock already demonstrated in r14s2, but remove recovery from forwarding-metadata next-state logic and capture load-repair eligibility in EX. These are inseparable parts of one MEM predictor-verification architecture: repair depends only on registered payloads, and correction must terminate in architectural squash/hold state rather than return through a forwarding-address mux. Keep the existing direction predictor, training-event register, four-entry fetch queue, arithmetic units and nret=1 contract.\n\nUse the current champion as the implementation base. EX prepares BRANCH/JAL/JALR packets and no longer produces recovery or predictor training. Carry the original predicted_taken bit, four predecoded branch predicates, two load-repair flags, source values, PC, and resolved direct/jump targets into EX/MEM. Preserve link PC+4 and full-width JALR addition with bit zero cleared. Resolve conditional outcome, conditional alignment fault and architectural branch pc_next in MEM. Remove the obsolete timed EX conditional comparator and all EX-versus-MEM recovery arbitration.\n\nSuppress the load-use bubble only for a positively decoded legal conditional branch (opcode BRANCH, funct3 in 0,1,4,5,6,7). All other load-use interlocks, including JALR, stay unchanged. Data backpressure still prevents the branch from advancing past an unaccepted older load. For each branch source, identify in EX whether the immediately older valid EX/MEM load has matching nonzero rd. This is the only stage that checks that identity for deferred repair.\n\nMake each saved repair flag mean that the older load WILL write that source when both instructions advance on this edge. Qualify the match with the older load's final write eligibility, including its exact memory-width/alignment trap decision. Reuse the MEM stage's existing post-trap reg_write qualification, or expose that single qualification signal to EX; do not duplicate an inconsistent legality rule. EX acceptance is already blocked until the load's bus transfer can complete. Capture the flag only with the branch's actual EX/MEM acceptance.\n\nFor a source matching that pending load, capture a fault-safe fallback: a matching retained MEM/WB register writer's value, otherwise the saved ID/EX operand. Never capture the load's arithmetic/address result as this fallback. Sources that do not match the pending load use ordinary EX forwarding. If the load traps, its repair flag is zero and the fallback is the architectural source. If it writes, the flag is one and the next-cycle MEM branch uses registered MEM/WB.wb_data. Apply independently to both operands. Retained WB control, not valid-gated writeback, qualifies the EX fallback.\n\nIn MEM the complete repair operation is saved_flag ? wb_data : saved_operand for each source. DO NOT perform another rd match, nonzero-rd test, reg_write qualification or valid test on this WB input. The immediately preceding load has just entered WB on the same edge that brought the branch into MEM; the branch's only added hold retains that very WB payload. Those facts establish the saved flag's meaning. Report these repaired values in RVFI. This removes WB eligibility/address comparisons from the late comparison/recovery cone without speculating about a load value or weakening trap handling.\n\nVerify all control transfers in MEM. Use the current balanced two-bit branch comparator and captured predicates, not seven replicated comparison lanes. Preserve actual legal aligned transfer semantics: a taken misaligned branch traps and resolves linearly to PC+4; a not-taken branch with an unaligned encoded target is legal; a faulting jump suppresses link write and resolves linearly. Direct-control mismatch compares the captured prediction with the actual eligible transfer. A legal aligned JALR is still unpredicted. Compute the correction candidate independently of the late condition: direct control uses predicted_taken ? PC+4 : saved_direct_target, and JALR uses its saved masked target. Architectural pc_next remains the actual resolved next PC. Illegal instructions never redirect or train.\n\nUse MEM as the sole recovery producer. A MEM control packet has no data-bus transaction, so unrelated io_dmemReady and younger execute_wait must not block verification. On a correct prediction, advance to WB normally while the younger EX instruction advances. On the first mismatch, assert correction, redirect IF and empty its entire queue, flush ID/EX with priority over any younger hold, retain the resolving EX/MEM packet, and clear MEM/WB.valid while retaining its other fields. Suppress all younger EX arithmetic requests and EX/MEM acceptance on this edge. This prevents a wrong-path load/store from reaching the memory interface and prevents a wrong-path multiply/divide from becoming an outstanding operation.\n\nSet recovery_applied_q on that first correction edge. On the next cycle, the same held packet advances once to WB and recovery_applied_q suppresses duplicate correction. Clear the bit when that packet actually advances; reset also clears it. The flushed EX bubble can replace the packet and the corrected fetch can enter ID/EX. Train a legal nonfaulting conditional branch exactly once when it advances to WB, through the existing registered IF training event; a correcting branch trains on this second edge. A correcting JAL/JALR link writes at its real, delayed WB retirement. This interlock retains the actual instruction until correction, rather than merely delaying RVFI, and must pass the existing unchanged liveness bound.\n\nCrucially, separate physical recovery holds from ordinary forwarding-metadata controls. Form ordinary controls from the existing load-use, instruction availability, data-bus and execution-wait conditions WITHOUT MEM correction. Feed these ordinary stall_id, stall_ex_mem and hold_mem_wb values to forward_unit. Feed the pipeline registers their physical controls, which additionally apply the MEM correction hold/flush described above. Do not reconnect correction to forward_unit through a combined hazard output, a younger execution cancellation term, a special recovery mux, or a 'next instruction' selector.\n\nOn the correction edge, the forwarding-match flops are allowed to capture values that do not describe the physically held EX/MEM and WB packets. This is safe for a specific, bounded reason: correction unconditionally makes the sole EX consumer invalid and clears all its controls. On the immediately following cycle, that bubble cannot redirect, train, launch arithmetic, access memory, write a register or retire. The resolving MEM branch never consults forward_unit; it uses its saved repair flags and the retained WB data. On the next acceptance edge, ordinary forwarding lookahead observes the real held producer registers and the corrected IF/ID head, and recomputes correct matches for the next live EX consumer. Reset retains its original priority. If the corrected head is absent or a new ordinary hold prevents its acceptance, EX remains an inert bubble and the metadata refresh continues until a live consumer is actually captured.\n\nPreserve original EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB forwarding priority and live producer write qualification. In particular, a held branch has reg_write=0, while an aligned correcting jump's link becomes a real WB producer on the advancement edge. The first target instruction must see that link normally. Do not delay producer data or general ALU execution; only the already-killed consumer's metadata is don't-care on the correction edge. Add assertions or directed monitors establishing that any metadata/control mismatch is confined to invalid EX bubbles, and that source selection for every valid consumer remains architecturally correct.\n\nThis leaves correction connected only to the IF PC/queue squash, ID/EX bubble, EX/MEM retention, WB-valid suppression, younger request kill and recovery-applied state. Specifically eliminate the r14s2 comparison-to-correction-to-forwarding-address-mux-to-match-flop path. Predictor counters, direct fetch targets, complete queue invalidation, runtime-reset history policy, memory lanes, arithmetic/ALTOPS schedules, decoder legality and top-level IO/RVFI remain unchanged. No additional prediction algorithm, cache or return stack is proposed.\n\nExpected fitness improvement is six percent with low confidence, not a measured result. The earlier MEM architecture saved 1.30 percent of CoreMark cycles. If that count is reproduced, acceptance above 221.24992 iterations/s requires more than 87.12265 MHz median Fmax. Recovering clock from the earlier 71.840 MHz result is a substantial requirement: removal of its measured metadata feedback and WB identity qualification must actually improve timing, while the general ALU and divider remain competing limits. The EX-only probe results above are negative evidence for simpler alternatives, not measurements of this final design. Report measured cycles and all scored Gowin placement options before claiming a gain.\n\nValidate the saved repair flags and recovery/metadata separation first. Exercise both repaired operands, every branch function, signed boundaries, a preceding trapping load and fallback to a retained WB writer. Force correction while a wrong-path MUL/DIV would request, while dmemReady or imemReady is low, and with a full fetch queue. Cover a correcting jump whose first target instruction reads its link, and correction immediately followed by DIV/REM. Assert zero younger side effects, exactly one correction/training/retirement as applicable, preserved WB repair data over the interlock, contiguous RVFI order and exact architectural operands/PCs/traps. Update only microarchitectural observers for the explicit MEM schedule; retain all architectural assertions. Run the complete unchanged fast formal suite including liveness, unique, reg, pc_fwd and pc_bwd, stalled reference cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks. Inspect vendor timing specifically for absence of correction in forwarding-match register input cones.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Extend EX/MEM with original prediction, captured branch predicates, two final load-repair flags and unambiguous saved jump/direct targets. Preserve all architectural fields and the IF packet layout.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Prepare control-transfer packets and fault-safe branch operands. Capture deferred load repair only after matching the immediately older load and checking its final write eligibility. Remove EX verification/recovery/training. On older MEM correction, hold the resolving EX/MEM packet and suppress every younger arithmetic request and capture.'},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Expose the current load final write eligibility to EX. Repair branch operands using only saved flags and registered WB data; resolve all control transfers and RVFI PCs/traps here. Implement sole MEM correction, recovery_applied_q, retained-WB first-edge suppression, and exactly-once final advancement/training.'},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Exempt only legal conditional-branch heads from the ordinary load-use bubble. Produce ordinary forwarding controls independently of MEM correction and distinct physical pipeline hold/flush controls with correction priority.'},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Ensure older MEM correction clears the whole ID/EX packet before any younger data/execution hold can retain it. Preserve normal capture, reset and non-recovery stall behavior.'},\n{'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'Define and consume only ordinary advancement/hold controls for metadata lookahead; exclude recovery and its derived kill/hold signals from all match-flop input cones. Preserve current matching logic, live producer qualification and refresh behavior. Document the invalid-consumer window and restoration before the next live consumer.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire the sole MEM recovery/training path, saved-load eligibility, registered WB repair data and distinct ordinary versus physical hazard controls. Forwarding receives ordinary controls; pipeline registers receive physical recovery controls. Preserve nret=1 and every external port.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Extend tests for MEM verification and recovery-before-retirement, final load-repair eligibility including trapping-load fallbacks, invalid-consumer metadata windows and correct first-target forwarding. Cover link consumers, reset, full queue, independent stalls, correction-to-DIV liveness, and exactly-once events with no wrong-path side effects. Retain all architectural checks and formal bounds.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=100))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and schema-valid:',p)\nprint('Fmax threshold with prior MEM cycles:',211.52*1.046*3937748/1e7)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
