{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_76", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r7s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-003-r7s2\ntitle: \"Gray-encoded bimodal counters with entry-local training\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion already predicts direct BRANCH/JAL and measures 4,201,709\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles at 57.319 MHz. Prior predictor trace replay\n  found about 51,854 conditional misses, with a 512-entry table removing\n  only about 12,000. Accuracy expansion alone is unlikely to clear the\n  required 4.6 percent fitness gain. The r5s2/r6s2 reset-removal experiments\n  show that predictor implementation cost can affect overall timing, but\n  neither establishes a gain on the current distributed-register-file\n  champion. The present counter update reads an indexed entry, performs\n  saturation, and writes it through a variable index; this can create a\n  shared lookup and broadcast feedback network for just 128 state bits.\nhypothesis: |\n  Recode the existing 64-entry, two-bit bimodal table as independent\n  Gray-encoded counter state machines with local feedback and a decoded\n  training enable. Encode strongly not-taken, weakly not-taken, weakly\n  taken and strongly taken as 00, 01, 11 and 10 respectively. The MSB\n  remains the predicted direction. Use a generate loop with constant\n  entry indices. Entry i resets to 2'b11 and updates only when\n  train_valid && train_index == i. For old bits h=counters[i][1],\n  l=counters[i][0] and resolved outcome t=train_taken, assign the new\n  bits simultaneously as h_next = l ? t : h and l_next = t ^ h.\n  These equations implement exactly the original saturating transition\n  graph without incrementers, decrementers or endpoint comparisons.\n  Read and update only that entry's own state in its clocked process:\n  no shared variable-index counter read or counter writeback mux belongs\n  in training. Keep counters[pc[7:2]][1] lookup and all interfaces intact.\n\n  This is a single predictor implementation hypothesis: reduce table\n  feedback, logic depth and routing pressure without changing prediction\n  accuracy, table capacity, reset lifetime, pipeline depth or arithmet\n…[1485 of 4557 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b53de161c8e6305d]…\nxpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the shared variable-index training process with 64 generated\n      constant-index Gray-counter processes. Use state order 00/01/11/10,\n      synchronous weakly-taken reset 11, a train_valid/train_index match\n      enable and the two local next-bit equations in the hypothesis.\n      Preserve array size, direction-MSB lookup, PC logic and every port.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all current assertions and add or extend a reference-model\n      check for all four logical counter states in both directions,\n      the Gray encoding's equivalence to binary saturation,\n      simultaneous lookup/training of the same index, alternating aliases,\n      repeated reset, and accepted versus held or squashed branches under\n      independent imem/dmem stalls. Check exact retirement PC sequences,\n      contiguous RVFI order, and no wrong-path store or divide effects.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
