{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat schemas/hypothesis.schema.json cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-002-r1s1 (improvement, +481.85%): Splitting shared DIV/REM into four clocked eight-step restoring iterations with separately registered normalization and correction breaks the full-width combinational EX dependency, making the global clock gain outweigh blocking divide latency in CoreMark.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-001-r2s0 (improvement, +27.81%): Reducing the restoring divider from eight to six steps per clock shortens the critical remainder_q feedback chain enough to outweigh two extra blocking cycles in CoreMark, with a final step[2] selection needed to process exactly 32 bits.\n- 2026-10-01 hyp-20261001-003-r2s2 (regression, +9.50%): Suppressing correct BRANCH/JAL r\n…[2209 of 5281 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3b3d5daafb3cf6ae]…\nistributed RAM attribution and mutually exclusive masked bypass/stored reads restores explicit ID/EX operand registers, removing the BSRAM-to-EX launch path and improving Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r7s0 (regression, -8.05%): Restricting MEM-to-EX load forwarding to branch comparisons does not preserve Fmax by itself: the prototype's 3.26% CoreMark cycle reduction was outweighed by its drop from 57.319 to 55.350 MHz.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r7s1 (improvement, +5.07%): A four-entry circular fall-through instruction queue improves CoreMark by using load-use, divide and data-memory holds to prefetch, then feeding decode during instruction-bus stalls without adding latency when the queue is empty.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r7s2 (regression, -4.41%): Carrying IF direct_target and PC+4 through 64 extra ID/EX bits removes EX adders but leaves divider remainder_q feedback critical, so unchanged CoreMark cycles cannot offset median Fmax falling from 57.319 to 54.790 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
