{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'], d.get('outcome'), d.get('fitness'), d.get('fmax_mhz'), d.get('cycles'))\n  print(d.get('title'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261001-003-r1s2 regression 13.53 5.685 4201709\nCompact bimodal branch prediction with immediate direct targets\nAdd one compact next-PC prediction mechanism to the existing combinational\nfetch/decode stage: a 64-entry table of two-bit saturating direction counters\nindexed by fetch PC[7:2], reset to weakly taken (2'b10). For a legal BRANCH\nencoding, use the counter MSB and compute its target directly from the\ncurrent instruction's sign-extended B immediate. Predict legal JAL taken\nusing its J immediate. Permit taken predictions only for word-aligned\ndirect targets. All other instructions, including JALR, predict PC+4.\nThis uses no target table, tags, return stack, or speculative history.\n\nAdvance the PC to the predicted next address only when the instruction is\naccepted into ID/EX. Carry a single predicted_taken bit alongside that\ninstruction, resetting it on bubbles and holding it with the entire ID/EX\npayload during stalls. A full predicted-target register is unnecessary:\nevery predicted target is a direct PC-relative target recomputed exactly\nin EX from the same instruction. Keep io_imemAddr driven by the PC register;\nprediction must not create a combinational instruction-memory address loop.\n\nResolve conditions and targets with the existing forwarded EX operands.\nFor direct branches and JAL, redirect only when predicted_taken differs\nfrom the actual legal, aligned transfer decision. A failed taken prediction\nredirects to the branch's PC+4; a missed taken prediction uses its resolved\ntarget. Unpredicted legal aligned JALR retains EX redirection and bit-zero\nmasking. Invalid instructions never redirect or train. Illegal encodings\nand misaligned targets retain existing trap, write suppression, and linear\nPC behavior. RVFI pc_next always records the architectural resolved next PC,\nindependently of prediction. Correct predictions must not flush fetch/ID.\n\nApply recovery and train the counter exactly once when a valid instruction\nadvances from EX into EX/MEM. Train only legal conditional branches without\na targ\n…[26773 of 29845 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8be049d60ab08291]…\nisks; the worst paths may remain in multiplication or division.\nThe projected six-percent gain is an unmeasured estimate with low\nconfidence. The failed local prototypes did not implement this\ntarget-carrying design and are not evidence of its timing or correctness.\nAcceptance requires more than 142.69532 iterations/s, approximately\n59.956421 MHz if measured cycles remain unchanged.\n\nPreserve nret=1 and every external port, decoder default-illegal\nbehavior, RVFI order and resolved PC semantics, imem/dmem handshakes,\nload-use bubbles, forwarding retention and blocking divider behavior.\nValidate unchanged prediction decisions, full-width target arithmetic\nincluding wraparound and alignment, both direction errors, JAL/JALR\nlinks, AUIPC, reset, independently stalled buses and wrong-path side\neffects. Run existing unit tests, all unchanged formal/cosimulation\ngates, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks and median Gowin\ntiming. No correctness or timing result is claimed for this final\nunimplemented proposal.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
