ining the demonstrated cycle reduction with this existing clock\\nimprovement is the most directly supported predictor experiment.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Add the previously validated 64-entry bimodal direct-control-flow\\npredictor to the current six-step-divider RTL. This is one predictor\\nchange: retain the current divider, ALU, forwarding, and pipeline depth.\\nDo not replace the current source with an earlier predictor snapshot\\nthat would also restore its older arithmetic implementation.\\n\\nUse 64 two-bit saturating counters indexed by fetch PC[7:2], reset to\\nweakly taken (2'b10). For a legal conditional BRANCH, predict taken when\\nthe counter MSB is set. Predict JAL taken unconditionally. Form the\\ndirect target from the full 32-bit fetch PC plus the instruction's\\\n…[19029 of 22101 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0cd1ffa1866bd77e]…\npipeline.py', 'description': 'Preserve existing architectural checks and adapt predictor fixtures\\nfor history retention across CPU resets. Use explicit training or\\nfresh simulations for tests requiring known counter values. Maintain\\nthe reference counter state across runtime reset and retain exact\\nsaturation, aliasing, pre-edge lookup and once-only training checks.\\nAdd repeated-reset tests with trained entries, reset during a would-be\\ntraining event, both recovery directions, independent bus stalls and\\nwrong-path memory/divide instructions. Assert architectural-state and\\nRVFI-order restart, retained predictor history, no reset-time training,\\nand no stale retirement or wrong-path side effects.\\n'}]}\n\n hyp-20261002-003-r7s2 Carry fetch-computed direct targets into predictor recovery regression {'fitness': 130.4, 'fmax_mhz': 54.79, 'cycles': 4201709}\n\n hyp-20261002-003-r8s2 Verify predicted conditional branches in MEM to absorb load-use bubbles broken {'fitness': None, 'fmax_mhz': None, 'cycles': None}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
